WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:05.623
Hope isn't this weak passive response.

2
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.010
It's actually one of the most powerful responses

3
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:09.963
that we can have.

4
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.530
Wisdom is the ability to think of the whole.

5
00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:19.810
Wisdom is the ability to think of the long-term consequences

6
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.790
of what we're doing.

7
00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.690 line:15%
Wisdom is returning

8
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.323 line:15%
to the natural intelligence of nature.

9
00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:34.650
It's no mystery that humanity is currently grappling

10
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.360
with a global pandemic, climate change

11
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.650
that is quickly becoming more and more experiential,

12
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:44.410
mass species extinction, and many other dire calamities

13
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.270
that can make humanity feel

14
00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.590
like it has lost its moral center.

15
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.850
Well, Jane Goodall,

16
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.070
the famed primatologist and climate activist,

17
00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.180
has a few thoughts about all of this.

18
00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.980
Thoughts that might surprise you.

19
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.540
Thoughts that don't revolve around what you might suspect

20
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.380
like cynicism or anger or pessimism,

21
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:08.633
but instead are all about hope.

22
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:12.040
But how is it that she can maintain hope

23
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.500
in the face of humanity's self-destructive trajectory?

24
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.300
I mean, what does hope even mean

25
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.210
and why is it desperately incumbent upon all of us

26
00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:26.160
to cultivate hope as a strategy to best evolve as humans

27
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.310
and a global community?

28
00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.960
Well, today's guest, Douglas Abrams

29
00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.980
wanted answers to these questions.

30
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.420
So he sought out Dame Goodall,

31
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:37.920
spent countless curious hours with her,

32
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.710
which culminated in this wonderful new book

33
00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.130
that he co-wrote with Jane called "The Book of Hope,"

34
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.360
which is this very intimate look

35
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.900
into not only the nature of hope itself,

36
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.840
but also into the heart and the mind of this woman,

37
00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.410
this woman who has truly revolutionized

38
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.730
how we view the natural world.

39
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.730
Being a former podcast guest and Stanford classmate of mine,

40
00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.633
Doug is a literary agent, he's an editor,

41
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:06.700
he's a New York Times best-selling author,

42
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.217
he's an impressive intellect,

43
00:02:08.217 --> 00:02:11.720
and a very charming and curious conversation partner

44
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.180
for today's exploration into the nature of hope,

45
00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.210
into the legacy of Jane Goodall

46
00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.770
and why indeed it is incumbent upon all of us

47
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:26.120
to shoulder an urgent but hopeful responsibility

48
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.180
for the future of our planet

49
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.420
and lead by the example set by Jane Goodall herself.

50
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:33.980
So here we go.

51
00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.523
This is me and Douglas Abrams.

52
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.340
Sweet, dude, good to see you.

53
00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.520
So great to see you.

54
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.460
It's been a long time.

55
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.160
I guess the last time we did this was 2017.

56
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:45.830
We were just saying before the podcast

57
00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.150
it seems like yesterday,

58
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.740
but it also feels like a complete past life.

59
00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.950
A whole 'nother world.

60
00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.900
I know, I know.

61
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:56.500
Well, here we are, the world is spinning off its axis.

62
00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:01.290
We are seeing unprecedented levels of partisanship

63
00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.830
and a breakdown in our ability to communicate

64
00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:04.760
in a healthy way

65
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:07.320
and we're having experiential climate change

66
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.730
and mass species extinction

67
00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:13.807
and the vitriol of social media inflaming all of it,

68
00:03:13.807 --> 00:03:16.540
and yet today, today, Douglas Abrams,

69
00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:20.130
we're gonna talk about hope, we're gonna talk about empathy,

70
00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.560
we're gonna talk about patience.

71
00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.010
I mean, where to even begin to unpack this?

72
00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:31.010 line:15%
Yeah, well, hope and despair are very close bedfellows.

73
00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:33.110 line:15%
And I think a lot of people are feeling

74
00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:34.320 line:15%
a lot of despair right now.

75
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.760
Yeah.
And it's in the despair

76
00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:39.710
that is where we need to find the hope.

77
00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:42.470
That was what was so interesting.

78
00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:46.460
I mean, in the book, you kind of play semi-antagonist

79
00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:51.460
or devil's advocate to Jane's unbridled optimism and hope

80
00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.325
as a means of like getting to the truth of what hope is.

81
00:03:55.325 --> 00:03:58.390
And I think there's a lot of misconceptions

82
00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.520
and misunderstandings around the word hope

83
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.960
and what it actually means,

84
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.680
specifically through her experience,

85
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.680
but in general, like how do you think of it then versus now,

86
00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.810
like you going into that project

87
00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.470
and what you've learned spending all that time with her

88
00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.820
about what hope truly is.

89
00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.580
Well, I think I was pretty cynical about hope

90
00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.680
when we started on the project,

91
00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.403
because I'm from New York and we don't really do hope.

92
00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:32.810
We do fear, we do anger, we do outrage.

93
00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.617
And I think hope can really sound like,

94
00:04:35.617 --> 00:04:37.050
"Well, let's hope for the best,"

95
00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:38.880
and it can sound really passive

96
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.483
and it can sound really Pollyanna and kind of unrealistic.

97
00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:49.470
And I think in going through this process with Jane

98
00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.870
and learning more about hope

99
00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.710
and also going and looking at the field of hope studies

100
00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.850
and understanding what sustains hope or what depletes hope,

101
00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.113
I think what I got clear on was that,

102
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.740
that hope isn't this kind of weak, passive response;

103
00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.850
it's actually one of the most powerful responses

104
00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:14.750
that we can have.

105
00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.690
And that there are really kinda three things that we do

106
00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.710
in relationship to the future.

107
00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.737
We either fantasize like,

108
00:05:21.737 --> 00:05:24.282
"I'm gonna be an NBA basketball player."

109
00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:25.115
Mm-hmm.
Hmm-mm.

110
00:05:25.115 --> 00:05:25.948
You are very tall.

111
00:05:25.948 --> 00:05:29.060
I am tall, but no eye-hand coordination.

112
00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:29.973
Not happening.

113
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.700
Or we dwell, which is what we do in New York a lot.

114
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.700
That's kinda the national pastime of my hometown.

115
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:41.620
Or we hope.

116
00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:45.090
And actually in hope,

117
00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.210
we are anticipating that there are gonna be obstacles

118
00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.890
and adversity along the way

119
00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:55.720
and we're not just kind of ignoring those.

120
00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.640
We're actually setting those goals

121
00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:02.090
and finding those realistic pathways to get to those goals.

122
00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.210
Yeah, there's this sense

123
00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.730
that hope is kind of a Pollyanna indulgence

124
00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.090
when in truth, it's like this active verb.

125
00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.900
And what really kind of triggered it for me

126
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.650
and helping me understand it

127
00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:18.400
is the idea of defining it by its opposite.

128
00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.020
Like the opposite of hope isn't necessarily despair,

129
00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.110
because despair is sort of a subset or a component of hope

130
00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:28.430
depending upon how you look at it,

131
00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.017
but that the opposite is apathy, right?

132
00:06:32.017 --> 00:06:34.390
And if you wanna actually take action,

133
00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:38.100
hope is kind of a requisite towards getting off the dime

134
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.130
to doing anything.

135
00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:40.963
Exactly.

136
00:06:40.963 --> 00:06:42.810
I mean, it's so easy to go from denial

137
00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.570
to despair really quickly,

138
00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.510
like just to go to the opposite, right?

139
00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:48.840
Whether it's about climate,

140
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.710
whether it's about political polarization,

141
00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:56.670
whatever you're kind of not paying attention to

142
00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.910
and then to go, "Oh God, this is so awful.

143
00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.420
We're never gonna solve this," and go to despair.

144
00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.020
And I think hope, whether it's at a global level

145
00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.460
or it's in our own life,

146
00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:10.550
is really about staying with the reality

147
00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:12.700
that life is filled with adversity

148
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.010
and suffering and challenge.

149
00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.920
But that there are, I mean, it was interesting to hear

150
00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:19.753
from the Hope Science

151
00:07:19.753 --> 00:07:23.010
that there are these kinda four components of hope:

152
00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:27.720
one being realistic goals, another being realistic pathways,

153
00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.720
another being a sense of agency or the ability to,

154
00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:34.440
and confidence to make things happen,

155
00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.100
and the last is social support.

156
00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:39.480
And those four things are really fundamental

157
00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.737
to any kind of enduring hope,

158
00:07:41.737 --> 00:07:44.881
and that hope has that necessity of action in it.

159
00:07:44.881 --> 00:07:45.714
Yeah.

160
00:07:45.714 --> 00:07:47.970
Am I the only one who thinks it's a little bit strange

161
00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.000
that there's something called hope studies?

162
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:51.760
Like that's just bizarre, right?

163
00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:52.593
I know, it's amazing.

164
00:07:52.593 --> 00:07:54.420
It was wild.
What is that about?

165
00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.560
So, I mean, I think, it was really interesting

166
00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.820
because to go to the science

167
00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.910
and find that there's actually researchers

168
00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:07.640
who study what increases hope or decreases hope

169
00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.620
and what is the, like,

170
00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.482
what are the outcomes of those people

171
00:08:11.482 --> 00:08:12.955
who have more or less hope

172
00:08:12.955 --> 00:08:14.000
and one of the things that was fascinating

173
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.610
with people with less hope

174
00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.630
were twice as likely to die in the next three years

175
00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:19.980
than people with more hope.

176
00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:24.380
Or in terms of the academic research,

177
00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:28.650
that hope was more important to academic success

178
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.650
than your IQ, than your grades, than your prior success.

179
00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:39.940
I mean, it's almost this little kind of fundamental secret

180
00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:42.540
of the human mind.

181
00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:44.140
And that was really interesting to hear.

182
00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.283
Jane call it this survival trait.

183
00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.440
That is what allows us to often make the difference

184
00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.490
between success and failure, life and death,

185
00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:58.320
and even potentially survival and extinction.

186
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.530
Does the science shed any a light

187
00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:05.530
on the nurture versus nature piece regarding hope?

188
00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.310
Like are people, you look at Jane and clearly she's born

189
00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:12.710
with a certain disposition towards hope

190
00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:16.340
and just this built-in level of resilience

191
00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.250
and almost obstinance that she's carried without her

192
00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.700
through her whole life.

193
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.000
But I have to believe that, much like resilience,

194
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.010
this is something that can be cultivated

195
00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:27.160
with intentional practice.

196
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.730
Well, actually I was really delighted,

197
00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:31.810
if that's the right word,

198
00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:36.140
to hear that Jane was kind of a weak and sickly child

199
00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:41.140
and didn't have that kind of buoyant, powerful optimism

200
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.180
and ability to kinda conquer the world,

201
00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.680
because I was kind of a weakened sickly child too.

202
00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:52.640
And I think she's a really amazing example of someone who,

203
00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:56.110
and also when you talk about nurture,

204
00:09:56.110 --> 00:10:00.080
like who had issue, who was actually challenged.

205
00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.627
Like she overheard her uncle who was a doctor say,

206
00:10:03.627 --> 00:10:08.260
"Oh, Jane wants to go work with wild animals in Africa.

207
00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.093
She'll never be able to do it,

208
00:10:09.093 --> 00:10:12.760
because she doesn't have the right constitution for it."

209
00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.695
And Jane was like, "Damn that," like, "I'm gonna"--

210
00:10:15.695 --> 00:10:16.528
Right.

211
00:10:16.528 --> 00:10:17.750
Right, but that's the piece, right?

212
00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.548
Like where does that come from?

213
00:10:19.548 --> 00:10:22.140
Does she come out of the womb with that

214
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:25.020
or is that a product of her upbringing?

215
00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:26.490
Yeah, I mean, I think one of the things

216
00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:28.250
we've talked about in the,

217
00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.230
so Jane has these four reasons for hope

218
00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:31.063
that we can talk about,

219
00:10:31.063 --> 00:10:32.760
and one of 'em is the power of young people,

220
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:37.060
and we talked about how do you nurture hope in young people,

221
00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:40.470
and I think, kind of what is that nurturing of resilience

222
00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:41.680
that you're talking about?

223
00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.220
And I do think it's, a lot of it is about

224
00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:50.822
encouraging kids to be able to struggle

225
00:10:50.822 --> 00:10:53.090
and learn from those struggles,

226
00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.150
allowing kids to struggle

227
00:10:55.150 --> 00:11:00.150
as well as being able to help them see

228
00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:04.810
that struggling is purposeful on the way to their goals.

229
00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:09.810
And so I think that it's having that those,

230
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.280
having goals, I mean, part of the challenge

231
00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.090
is a lot of people don't have goals or they don't have,

