00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:21.020
and a lot of people don't have the same level of pathways

234
00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:23.110
to realize those goals,

235
00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:28.060
but I think that we can encourage hope in young people

236
00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:30.420
by helping them to identify those goals

237
00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:31.253
that drive them forward.

238
00:11:31.253 --> 00:11:34.810
She read "Tarzan" and she read,

239
00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:37.667
and she read "Dr. Doolittle," and she was like,

240
00:11:37.667 --> 00:11:39.350
"I wanna do that."

241
00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:42.840
That it was kinda really the spark of the imagination.

242
00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.080
Yeah, well, setting goals is important,

243
00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.403
but setting, like you said earlier,

244
00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:51.750
somewhat reasonable goals, right?

245
00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.540
So if you have a young person and you set them on a path

246
00:11:54.540 --> 00:11:56.520
towards working to something that's doable

247
00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.910
but is gonna require some rigor and discipline and whatnot

248
00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.890
and then they achieve that,

249
00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:05.370
that engenders a little self-esteem,

250
00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.280
it also creates that sense of agency.

251
00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:10.300
So all of those play into

252
00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:12.890
like the burgeoning sense of hope, right?

253
00:12:12.890 --> 00:12:15.710
So hope almost works like this umbrella

254
00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.780
for these other personality assets

255
00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.713
that help drive you forward and shape like a worldview.

256
00:12:22.586 --> 00:12:23.419
Exactly.

257
00:12:23.419 --> 00:12:28.110
I think that it was so powerful to see that hope is,

258
00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.030
so one of the things that kinda blew my mind

259
00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:32.920
was finding out from a neuroscientist

260
00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:36.270
that hope originates in the frontal,

261
00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:39.870
in the prefrontal cortex, which is the kind of front of,

262
00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.173
between our eyes, where we have,

263
00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:48.920
where we also have language, where we have time travel

264
00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:50.500
or the ability to imagine the future

265
00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:52.200
and where we have problem solving.

266
00:12:53.382 --> 00:12:57.000
Hope seems to be like this kind of component

267
00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:59.530
of the human imagination

268
00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:03.880
to envision something better than what is now.

269
00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.697
And as one of the researchers said,

270
00:13:05.697 --> 00:13:07.970
"We are hope/fear creatures."

271
00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:12.110
So we are either operating out of that part of our brain

272
00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:14.530
which can hope and imagine the future

273
00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:17.510
and work toward those goals and find those pathways,

274
00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:20.970
or we're in kinda the more ancient parts of our brain

275
00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:24.920
where we're in fear or kind of frozen

276
00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:26.750
with a sense that there is no hope.

277
00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.840
Yeah, well, the tectonic plates of culture right now

278
00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:33.830
are working over time to push us towards the fear side--

279
00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:35.140
Absolutely.
That balance.

280
00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:38.770
And that's enough to feel despair.

281
00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:40.990
But perhaps within that despair,

282
00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:44.430
we can see the seedlings of hope, I don't know.

283
00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.263
It's hard.

284
00:13:45.263 --> 00:13:46.096
It is hard.

285
00:13:46.096 --> 00:13:48.250
I mean, and obviously there are a lot of things

286
00:13:48.250 --> 00:13:50.610
to feel despairing about.

287
00:13:50.610 --> 00:13:53.510
But as Jane said, "Without hope, there really is no hope."

288
00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.370
It becomes this self-fulfilling prophecy, right?

289
00:13:57.370 --> 00:14:01.310
And I would say, the merchants of cynicism and doom

290
00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:06.220
would love us to just kind of say, "Well, forget it.

291
00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:08.680
We're not gonna be able to solve our climate crisis,"

292
00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:13.680
or, "Our country is kind of inevitably going to be divided

293
00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.160
in the way it is."

294
00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:18.620
And I think that there's kind of vested interests

295
00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.530
in those positions actually.

296
00:14:21.530 --> 00:14:24.860
And I think that hoping for something else

297
00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:26.840
and actively moving forward,

298
00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:28.470
and I think that's the decisive piece,

299
00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:31.230
which is that real hope, as I learned from Jane,

300
00:14:31.230 --> 00:14:34.450
is really taking action.

301
00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:36.030
It's not a passive response.

302
00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:37.340
And if you're doing that,

303
00:14:37.340 --> 00:14:41.010
then you're fundamentally challenging the status quo

304
00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.860
in some way that can be threatening to vested interest.

305
00:14:44.860 --> 00:14:46.301
Right.

306
00:14:46.301 --> 00:14:49.070
And those vested interests are also kind of harboring

307
00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:50.530
a perverted notion of hope.

308
00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:54.350
Like they know fear travels quickly, fear is profitable

309
00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:58.190
so they're hopeful about their balance sheets.

310
00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:59.960
You know what I mean?

311
00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:03.139
Like their hope is just directed in the wrong way.

312
00:15:03.139 --> 00:15:04.730
So you can be publicly despairing

313
00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.760
because you're building some kind of platform

314
00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:11.710
premised upon that that will enrich you in a certain way,

315
00:15:11.710 --> 00:15:13.340
in a self-serving way, right?

316
00:15:13.340 --> 00:15:14.850
So how do you redirect that hope?

317
00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:19.550
I mean, there's a misalignment of social incentives

318
00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:23.280
to direct hope into healthier,

319
00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.480
into healthier trajectories for,

320
00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:28.020
that are more communally oriented.

321
00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:28.853
Absolutely.

322
00:15:28.853 --> 00:15:31.020
And there's this wonderful saying

323
00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:35.973
that every cynic is like a frustrated optimist.

324
00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:43.707
It's very easy to get pushed into that response of like,

325
00:15:43.707 --> 00:15:45.700
"It's safer to be a cynic.

326
00:15:45.700 --> 00:15:48.790
It's safer to be kind of,"

327
00:15:48.790 --> 00:15:51.130
I mean, hoping is vulnerable.

328
00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.690
Yeah, and it's sort of considered more intellectual

329
00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:55.970
to be cynical, right?

330
00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:58.200
And true cynicism is lazy.

331
00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:03.200
And it is less safe to be vulnerable in that way.

332
00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:04.813
Exactly.

333
00:16:04.813 --> 00:16:06.260
I mean, to want something to,

334
00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:09.350
whether it's for your own life or for the world,

335
00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:12.130
is a vulnerable place 'cause you might not get it.

336
00:16:12.130 --> 00:16:14.680
But honestly, the alternative,

337
00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:19.680
which is kind of like despaired,

338
00:16:19.720 --> 00:16:24.720
cynical kind of satisfaction, you're a lot in life.

339
00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:28.578
In some ways it's like, "Wow, okay.

340
00:16:28.578 --> 00:16:33.578
Maybe it feels more comfortable in some ways

341
00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:36.460
or more self-protective."

342
00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:39.050
But ultimately, the whole,

343
00:16:39.050 --> 00:16:40.260
I mean, this was really fascinating,

344
00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:41.150
working with Jane, right,

345
00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:43.880
because here's somebody who's studied human origins

346
00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:48.420
and animal nature and like we,

347
00:16:48.420 --> 00:16:51.810
hope was part of what allowed humans to evolve

348
00:16:51.810 --> 00:16:53.650
and to become what we are today,

349
00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.550
to become, create this global civilization.

350
00:16:56.550 --> 00:16:59.710
If we hadn't been, had that capacity

351
00:16:59.710 --> 00:17:02.500
to hope for something different or something better,

352
00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:03.490
it never would've happened.

353
00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:04.840
Right, right.

354
00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:08.270
So you begin the process of writing this book,

355
00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.380
from what I can understand in the book,

356
00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:12.160
which is wonderful by the way, I loved it.

357
00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:13.920
Thank you.

358
00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:17.470
While you were putting together "The Book of Joy," right,

359
00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:21.110
because you were kind of visiting with those fellows

360
00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:22.760
and then catching Jane on the way,

361
00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:25.330
like these were sort of happening somewhat,

362
00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:27.640
like you were beginning the conversations with Jane

363
00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:29.730
while you were also writing "The Book of Joy,"

364
00:17:29.730 --> 00:17:31.720
is that kind of how the timeline played out?

365
00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.530
So actually I had finished writing "The Book of Joy."

366
00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:37.650
We were working on the "Mission: Joy" movie

367
00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:39.970
based on "The Book of Joy" with the Dalai Lama

368
00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:40.803
and Desmond Tutu,

369
00:17:40.803 --> 00:17:45.500
but I had approached Jane about working together

370
00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.627
and she basically gave us the green light and said,

371
00:17:48.627 --> 00:17:52.320
"Yes, come to Tanzania like in two weeks,"

372
00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:56.080
and so we had to kind of scramble and develop

373
00:17:56.080 --> 00:17:59.120
all of the questions and the ideas for the book

374
00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:00.890
and to go and interview her.

375
00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:05.440
And then that was two years ago, in August.

376
00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:07.710
And then while I was in Tanzania,

377
00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:09.550
I got the phone call from my wife

378
00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:12.250
that my dad had gone into the hospital

379
00:18:12.250 --> 00:18:13.090
and that it looked really serious.

380
00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:15.610
So I had to actually leave and come back

381
00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.830
and be with him for two months

382
00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:21.870
while he was actually dealing

383
00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.550
with T-cell lymphoma and dying.

384
00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:28.960
And then we, so through the grieving process

385
00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:33.410
of watching him die and what he called companioning him

386
00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:38.170
on his mighty journey to death and then being,

387
00:18:38.170 --> 00:18:39.480
and I was flying back and forth

388
00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.570
between New York where he was dying

389
00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:45.040
and California where my son had a traumatic brain injury

390
00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.330
and kind of, as you know, going back and forth

391
00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.520
between those two routes, and working on the book,

392
00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:52.370
and talking to Jane in the midst of that,

393
00:18:52.370 --> 00:18:55.920
so it really was, at first it was kind of theoretical

394
00:18:55.920 --> 00:19:00.680
and it was like hope and, as a concept,

395
00:19:00.680 --> 00:19:05.120
and then I think when you have your own challenges

396
00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:07.680
that you face, like the illness of a loved one

397
00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:10.320
or grief and loss of a loved one,

398
00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:11.950
it becomes much more personal.

399
00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:12.783
Yeah, yeah.

400
00:19:12.783 --> 00:19:15.210
I mean, the juxtaposition of those heavy things

401
00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:20.210
with this subject matter, I think it gets illustrated

402
00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:26.060
in the richness in which you deal with these themes

403
00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:27.830
because they're so challenging.

404
00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:32.830
And they become complex because of our own life experiences

405
00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:36.050
with our loved ones and with ourselves.

406
00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.850
But I think what's interesting here,

407
00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:41.880
was it hope that drew you to Jane

408
00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:45.650
or was it trying to figure out how to do a book with Jane?

409
00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:47.330
Because you have "The Book of Joy,"

410
00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:48.850
now we have "The Book of Hope"

411
00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:52.030
and this is gonna be this ongoing Global Icons Series,

412
00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:53.410
which is really cool,

413
00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:57.940
but on some level they're sort of subject matter vehicles

414
00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:00.330
to reimagine what a memoir could be.

415
00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.290
Because you're telling Jane's story,

416
00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:05.050
but you're doing it through the lens of this specific idea

417
00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:08.060
as opposed to calling the book

418
00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:09.780
the life story of Jane Goodall or whatever

419
00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:13.900
and just doing a linear treatment of this remarkable life.

420
00:20:13.900 --> 00:20:17.698
Yeah, and the idea is, for the Global Icons,

421
00:20:17.698 --> 00:20:20.580
as said there, these kind of travel log dialogues,

422
00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:24.180
so it's like the reader gets to come along and either,

423
00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.440
with "The Book of Joy," have this kinda encounter

424
00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:28.100
with the Dalai Lama and Desmond Tutu--

425
00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:31.099
Very long series of long-winded conversations.

426
00:20:31.099 --> 00:20:33.360
(Doug laughs)

427
00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:36.910
With which, in Jane's case, involves building fires

428
00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:38.980
and going on long walks and drinking whiskey.

429
00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:40.143
You're right, exactly.

430
00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:43.890
Hopefully not too long-winded.

431
00:20:43.890 --> 00:20:47.220
But you definitely, to get to go

432
00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:51.160
and kinda have the experience and get to sip the whiskey,

433
00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:54.040
which I actually had to train for,

434
00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:58.100
because I had heard that she was a serious whiskey drinker.

435
00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.190
And I had to practice a little.

436
00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:02.393
Get my Johnny Walker handled.

437
00:21:05.482 --> 00:21:06.790
But so the idea of the series is,

438
00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:11.220
yeah, that there are these kind of really amazing icons

439
00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:14.680
and what, but to reveal their humanity, right?

440
00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:19.390
So not just kind of them as some kind of vaunted,

441
00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:24.610
laudatory figure gonna, on the global stage

442
00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.380
that we all say, "Wow, isn't that incredible,"

443
00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.110
but to really get inside their own heart and mind

444
00:21:31.110 --> 00:21:32.660
and their own experiences.

445
00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:34.960
And that's been the most rewarding part, I think,

446
00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:37.520
of both "The Book of Joy" and "The Book of Hope"

447
00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:39.623
is people say that it gives them,

448
00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:43.210
they see these people in their experience,

449
00:21:43.210 --> 00:21:45.320
they relate to them in a whole different way

450
00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:48.330
because they're relating to their humanity.

451
00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:51.833
And I think that, in this case,

452
00:21:53.030 --> 00:21:56.910
so we went to, we knew we wanted to work with Jane,

453
00:21:56.910 --> 00:22:00.180
and as somebody who has like incredible insight

454
00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:03.140
into human nature and into,

455
00:22:03.140 --> 00:22:04.750
has spent her life studying nature,

456
00:22:04.750 --> 00:22:09.480
and as clear, she's been this global messenger of hope

457
00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:12.800
and traveled the world, like prior to the pandemic,

458
00:22:12.800 --> 00:22:17.590
300 days a year trying to spread hope and understand hope.

459
00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:20.557
So it became really clear that that was like,

460
00:22:20.557 --> 00:22:22.610
"Who's the best person in the world,"

461
00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:24.570
like if you're gonna understand joy,

462
00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:26.270
you're gonna go to the Dalai Lama and Desmond Tutu

463
00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:27.240
to understand it.

464
00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:28.490
If you're gonna understand hope,

465
00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:30.570
Jane was clearly the person we needed to talk to.

466
00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:31.403
Right.

467
00:22:31.403 --> 00:22:34.080
This is something that she's talked about all the time.

468
00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:36.320
And it's been fascinating,

469
00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.670
in the wake of the book coming out

470
00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:41.570
and her kind of embracing the press aspect

471
00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:44.730
of pushing it out into the world, to like see her all,

472
00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:48.458
like she's relentless and she's 87 now, like 80--

473
00:22:48.458 --> 00:22:49.566
87, amazing, yeah.

474
00:22:49.566 --> 00:22:51.390
It's like just appearing everywhere

475
00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:55.850
and just totally on point, like doesn't miss a beat,

476
00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.640
so vital, like so present, so passionate.

477
00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:02.430
This is somebody who's been doing this their whole life

478
00:23:02.430 --> 00:23:05.680
and started talking about these issues back in the '80s

479
00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:08.107
when nobody wanted to hear about it

480
00:23:08.107 --> 00:23:11.200
and has been banging the drum ever since.

481
00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:14.150
As you said, like 300 cities a year until COVID,

482
00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:17.480
and then it just becomes a Zoom bonanza for her.

483
00:23:17.480 --> 00:23:19.540
You would think that she would fatigue over this

484
00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:21.540
or that she would get frustrated,

485
00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:25.660
and that's a Testament to this level of hope

486
00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:29.620
that despite her continually putting out this message

487
00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:33.800
thousands of times that she still has to do it

488
00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:35.700
and that she's gonna work until she dies.

489
00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:40.700
And she remains just as enthusiastic about her advocacy

490
00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:43.520
as she ever was.

491
00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:47.190
It's so cool and inspiring, honestly,

492
00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:52.190
to see these people in their 80s and even 90s now

493
00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:53.853
or just turned 90,

494
00:23:54.730 --> 00:23:58.270
we're actually working with 101-year-old woman

495
00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.900
who founded the field of holistic medicine

496
00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.840
and integrative health, it's like,

497
00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:05.560
who has a 10-year plan by the way--

498
00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:08.010
What's her name?
Her name is Gladys McGarry.

499
00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:10.347
Wow.

500
00:24:10.347 --> 00:24:11.180
Yeah, it's amazing.

501
00:24:11.180 --> 00:24:12.930
And it's just like these,

502
00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:15.680
the people that you find who are most vital

503
00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:17.770
at that stage of life are these people

504
00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:20.540
who are really living for something

505
00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.200
more than their own aches and pains.

506
00:24:22.200 --> 00:24:24.780
They've got something that they're super passionate about.

507
00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:26.530
They feel that they can contribute and give.

508
00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:31.530
And Jane has blown me away with her incredible dedication

509
00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:35.890
and her passion and her energy and her ability to,

510
00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:38.800
she says she's more in busier now in the pandemic

511
00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:40.490
than ever before.
Right, I know.

512
00:24:40.490 --> 00:24:44.300
And like she's just doing, and it was amazing.

513
00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:45.370
And I think the world,

514
00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.710
it's like the world has kinda caught up with Jane.

515
00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:49.607
As you said, she was in the '80s

516
00:24:49.607 --> 00:24:50.970
and she had beating the drum

517
00:24:50.970 --> 00:24:52.430
when nobody was really listening,

518
00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:56.323
and I think as, sadly, we're becoming,

519
00:24:57.760 --> 00:25:02.160
gratefully, but sadly the realities of climate

520
00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:04.830
and the natural disaster

521
00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:07.140
that we've been creating on our earth

522
00:25:07.140 --> 00:25:09.500
is so much more clear to people.

523
00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:12.800
And so I think people have caught up,

524
00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.350
So there's a reason that Jane is now on the cover

525
00:25:15.350 --> 00:25:16.183
of Time Magazine.

526
00:25:16.183 --> 00:25:17.740
It's like we need her.

527
00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:19.100
It's like the Jane moment.

528
00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:20.380
We need her.

529
00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:23.730
We need both the sounding of the alarm,

530
00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:27.480
but we also need the steady hand of the recognition.

531
00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.880
And I think what was powerful to hear in the book,

532
00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:35.040
in writing the book with her was this sense of,

533
00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:37.890
this kinda grounded sense of our ability

534
00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:39.850
to handle great challenges

535
00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:42.220
like in World War II, for example.

536
00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:47.020
And that we kind of lulled ourselves into this complacency

537
00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:49.290
that like life should just be easy.

538
00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:53.110
And like if life is hard or there are challenges

539
00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:54.873
or the world is faced with crisis,

540
00:25:54.873 --> 00:25:57.010
that there's something wrong

541
00:25:57.010 --> 00:25:59.240
as opposed to like, "That's how human life is."

542
00:25:59.240 --> 00:26:01.320
This is the way that it is.
Yeah, exactly.

543
00:26:01.320 --> 00:26:06.060
Yeah, there's that story of her

544
00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.510
coming up against some fatigue

545
00:26:08.510 --> 00:26:12.830
with the relentlessness of the interviews and the speaking

546
00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:16.540
and there's that matchbook box thing that she created

547
00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:19.380
when she was a little girl around confirmation.

548
00:26:19.380 --> 00:26:21.330
And then in each little drawer,

549
00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:23.350
there's a tiny little scroll

550
00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:26.420
where she had inscribed something from the Bible

551
00:26:26.420 --> 00:26:27.800
or something like that.

552
00:26:27.800 --> 00:26:32.407
She pulls it out and it was some passage about like,

553
00:26:32.407 --> 00:26:35.760
"You cannot, you have to continue to sow the field,"

554
00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:39.990
or whatever and just okay, off you go.

555
00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:42.983
Yeah, I mean, I think that sense of like,

556
00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:47.050
her favorite quote is actually one, from the Bible,

557
00:26:47.050 --> 00:26:52.050
which is like, "As your days, so shall your strength be,"

558
00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:53.080
or something like that,

559
00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:56.970
which is just like you're given just enough

560
00:26:56.970 --> 00:27:00.380
to sustain you through the challenges that you face.

561
00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:04.947
Yeah, and there's so much to be gleaned from understanding

562
00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:09.320
that her ability to show up and be that vital

563
00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:11.390
is so rooted to her passion

564
00:27:11.390 --> 00:27:13.900
and her deep sense of purpose, right?

565
00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:15.410
Like if you can anchor yourself

566
00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:17.810
in something that you care about deeply

567
00:27:17.810 --> 00:27:21.910
and channel it in a way that is in service to others,

568
00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:24.210
like it gets her up out of bed every morning,

569
00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:26.500
it keeps her invigorated about life

570
00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:30.840
and cognitively, if nothing else, like it's just remarkable.

571
00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:31.673
Right.

572
00:27:31.673 --> 00:27:34.150
So I think that that sense of purpose is huge.

573
00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:36.340
I was also thinking about how,

574
00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:38.420
to come back to the conversation about nurturing,

575
00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:41.830
like it's amazing, like it was kind of mind-blowing

576
00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:45.900
that her mother picked up and went with her

577
00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:50.900
into the African forest, into Gombe to do the research

578
00:27:51.170 --> 00:27:53.600
because they wouldn't let a white woman--

579
00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:54.433
Right, she needed a chaperone

580
00:27:54.433 --> 00:27:55.266
She needed a chaperone.

581
00:27:55.266 --> 00:27:56.250
They wouldn't let a white woman

582
00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.790
kinda go into the forest back then.

583
00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:01.723
And like that's kind of incredible.

584
00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:05.253
And I think one of the things that,

585
00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:08.930
that sense of, one of the things that,

586
00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:10.560
most interesting things I learned about hope

587
00:28:10.560 --> 00:28:14.570
is that, as one researcher described it, it's a social gift.

588
00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:16.270
It's something we give to each other.

589
00:28:16.270 --> 00:28:20.280
We encourage and support each other's sense of hope.

590
00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.380
And obviously our parents do that

591
00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.890
immensely for us as children,

592
00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:27.070
but we do that for one another

593
00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:29.150
when we're facing those obstacles,

594
00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:32.330
that social support and that sense of hope.

595
00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.050
And that was also really interesting,

596
00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:36.750
that hope and hopelessness are contagious.

597
00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:38.670
That this sense of like

598
00:28:38.670 --> 00:28:41.420
we think we're all these separate kind of minds

599
00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:46.420
and separate people, but then our sense of hopefulness

600
00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:48.430
or despair about the future

601
00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:50.140
deeply impacts everybody else's.

602
00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:51.660
Right, right, right, yeah.

603
00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:54.360
That's a really powerful idea.

604
00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.790
I can't help but think that,

605
00:28:56.790 --> 00:28:59.190
you get into this section later in the book

606
00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:02.100
about spirituality and faith,

607
00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:05.420
and I'm interested in how faith differs

608
00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:07.870
from hope technically,

609
00:29:07.870 --> 00:29:10.660
but there's this sense that I can't escape

610
00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:12.480
of like predestination with her.

611
00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:16.010
I mean, that tracks all the way back to the beech tree

612
00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:17.160
and her climbing the tree

613
00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:20.910
and just this deep-rooted connection to the natural world

614
00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:25.220
that's almost preternatural for a young person to have,

615
00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:28.820
that she remains true to her entire life,

616
00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.430
like her interest never wavered.

617
00:29:31.430 --> 00:29:32.410
And on some level,

618
00:29:32.410 --> 00:29:35.160
like obviously when you look in the rear view mirror,

619
00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.550
it's 2020, but it all adds up perfectly.

620
00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:39.090
It's like, "Of course this is the life

621
00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:41.020
that this person lived."

622
00:29:41.020 --> 00:29:43.580
And along the way, there were these amazing

623
00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:45.860
kind of synchronicities and coincidences,

624
00:29:45.860 --> 00:29:49.360
and the fact that she even ended up being,

625
00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:51.210
doing secretarial work for Leakey

626
00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:53.910
and like what kind of came after that,

627
00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:56.410
like, you couldn't script that.

628
00:29:56.410 --> 00:29:58.370
Like if you put that in a narrative movie,

629
00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:00.070
it just would be too unbelievable.

630
00:30:01.410 --> 00:30:03.980
Well, it's funny, like I totally get that

631
00:30:03.980 --> 00:30:07.070
and I think, from one vantage point, that's totally true.

632
00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:10.270
And from the other vantage point, it's this like,

633
00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:12.670
it's exactly what you said about hindsight being 2020,

634
00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:16.620
it's like we all live these lives

635
00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:20.550
and from day to day we don't understand where they're going

636
00:30:20.550 --> 00:30:23.730
or what the meaning of our life is.

637
00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:25.947
And then in retrospect, we're like,

638
00:30:25.947 --> 00:30:28.163
"Oh yeah, like I get it.

639
00:30:28.163 --> 00:30:30.480
Like that led me to this, to that."

640
00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:33.740
My dad had this wonderful expression when he was dying.

641
00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:37.910
He said, "It's all part of my curriculum."

642
00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:39.220
Right.

643
00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:40.910
It's such an amazing perspective.

644
00:30:40.910 --> 00:30:42.670
It's such an amazing perspective, right?

645
00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:44.750
Because whether you believe it's predestined

646
00:30:44.750 --> 00:30:47.050
or you believe in God or you don't believe in God

647
00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:49.710
or you believe that there's purpose in life or not,

648
00:30:49.710 --> 00:30:52.210
you can make it meaningful in your own life

649
00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:54.830
by seeing it as part of your own curriculum.

650
00:30:54.830 --> 00:30:57.710
And I think that Jane,

651
00:30:57.710 --> 00:31:01.340
Jane definitely took a lot of botany and biology

652
00:31:01.340 --> 00:31:02.420
kind of in her early,

653
00:31:02.420 --> 00:31:06.043
like if you wanna kinda say what her curriculum was,

654
00:31:06.043 --> 00:31:08.890
like she definitely fell in love with the natural world,

655
00:31:08.890 --> 00:31:10.610
but lots of people read "Tarzan,"

656
00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.910
lots of people read "Dr. Doolittle,"

657
00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:17.910
and what I think made her an amazing example

658
00:31:18.250 --> 00:31:23.071
of this kind of active hope is that then she said,

659
00:31:23.071 --> 00:31:25.850
"I'm gonna keep pushing myself

660
00:31:25.850 --> 00:31:27.120
closer to my goals.

661
00:31:27.120 --> 00:31:29.010
I'm gonna find those pathways," right?

662
00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:33.030
I mean, so yes, is it amazing and miraculous.

663
00:31:33.030 --> 00:31:35.500
And could we have scripted it in a Hollywood movie

664
00:31:35.500 --> 00:31:37.920
that she would go work with Louis Leakey,

665
00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:40.640
who would then be wanting to understand

666
00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:45.480
our ape-like ancestors and send her into the jungle?

667
00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:50.480
But she had to get her way to that secretarial training.

668
00:31:50.483 --> 00:31:53.530
She had to find her way to his office.

669
00:31:53.530 --> 00:31:56.460
She had to impress him on the digs that she,

670
00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:59.463
and how she dealt with the lion and the rhinoceros,

671
00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:02.570
like she had to convince him

672
00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:04.860
that she was the one for the job.

673
00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:05.882
Right.

674
00:32:05.882 --> 00:32:08.770
I think in some way we're all, in our own curriculum,

675
00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:13.370
trying to prove, like, to ourself,

676
00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:15.100
that we're up for the task.

677
00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:16.300
Yeah.

678
00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:19.130
Of course, the story of her being in Gombe

679
00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:20.790
and spending all the time with the chimps

680
00:32:20.790 --> 00:32:24.450
and it all being documented is so well-told,

681
00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:28.750
but I didn't know a lot of the details of her life story

682
00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:31.740
and I truly did not appreciate

683
00:32:31.740 --> 00:32:35.930
just the level of resistance that she faced

684
00:32:35.930 --> 00:32:39.240
and the kind of state of science at the time,

685
00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:44.210
this idea that perhaps we were not evolved from apes

686
00:32:44.210 --> 00:32:48.340
and these animals did not have an interior emotional life

687
00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:49.860
and there was nothing to be learned or gleaned

688
00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.490
by observing them.

689
00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:54.070
And her, back to that kind of obstinance

690
00:32:54.070 --> 00:32:58.510
or this very directed sense of purpose,

691
00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:00.140
to put herself in that position

692
00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:02.200
and then have the patience to literally be there

693
00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:06.880
like 10 years in order to take what she was observing

694
00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:10.020
and then with Leakey's help, contextualize it

695
00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:12.870
in a vernacular that it could be even received

696
00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:14.230
by the scientific community,

697
00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:15.880
I mean, the barriers and the obstacles

698
00:33:15.880 --> 00:33:17.750
and the resistance was unbelievable.

699
00:33:17.750 --> 00:33:19.067
Unbelievable.

700
00:33:19.067 --> 00:33:20.870
I mean, to remember, first of all, that she--

701
00:33:20.870 --> 00:33:22.080
And being a woman on top of that.

702
00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:23.285
Exactly.

703
00:33:23.285 --> 00:33:24.772
Yeah, that she was a woman at a time when,

704
00:33:24.772 --> 00:33:28.050
as she says, "Men weren't even going into the forest

705
00:33:28.050 --> 00:33:29.107
to study animals."

706
00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:33.100
She didn't even have an undergraduate degree, right,

707
00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:34.960
and let alone a PhD, which he later got.

708
00:33:34.960 --> 00:33:36.062
Right.

709
00:33:36.062 --> 00:33:37.770
She didn't even have an undergrad degree.

710
00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:40.140
She literally had a secretarial training course

711
00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:44.040
and she's going into the jungle and having to--

712
00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:44.943
With her mom, at first.

713
00:33:44.943 --> 00:33:46.903
With her mom as a chaperone.

714
00:33:48.040 --> 00:33:49.160
And God bless moms, and--

715
00:33:49.160 --> 00:33:52.110
After this proper upbringing in England,

716
00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:54.140
where it was all about finding

717
00:33:54.140 --> 00:33:57.420
a rich guy to marry and debutantes and all that kinda stuff.

718
00:33:57.420 --> 00:33:58.740
Right, exactly.

719
00:33:58.740 --> 00:34:02.443
It definitely was, like it was the path less taken.

720
00:34:03.720 --> 00:34:06.480
But that determination that she was gonna fall,

721
00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:09.230
and she had these goals, she was gonna follow them,

722
00:34:09.230 --> 00:34:10.830
she was gonna find out pathways.

723
00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:13.580
I mean, the story that she tells

724
00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:18.580
about how the guy who drove them from Kenya to Gombe

725
00:34:19.840 --> 00:34:22.850
said he'd never thought he'd see them again alive.

726
00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:27.423
And she just really just kind of,

727
00:34:28.340 --> 00:34:30.810
she was very lucky obviously.

728
00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:33.140
There were a lot of dangers that she was facing

729
00:34:33.140 --> 00:34:36.350
that made her life really dramatic

730
00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.480
and more dramatic than most of ours.

731
00:34:38.480 --> 00:34:40.670
But to come back to the point of what you were saying

732
00:34:40.670 --> 00:34:43.010
about like what she did for us

733
00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:46.140
in terms of changing our understanding of humanity, right,

734
00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:50.163
there's, I mean, this whole relationship

735
00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:54.540
to the natural world that obviously we've lost so much of

736
00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:58.210
that we are animals and that we can't survive

737
00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:01.340
without the ecosystem and without nature

738
00:35:01.340 --> 00:35:02.833
and that we are nature.

739
00:35:04.010 --> 00:35:08.850
She was one of the first people to help bridge that gap

740
00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:12.860
and help us see that, yeah, animals have personalities,

741
00:35:12.860 --> 00:35:16.370
have emotions, that this is a continuum of intelligence.

742
00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:18.957
And I asked her, "Do animals have hope?"

743
00:35:19.871 --> 00:35:22.550
And she said, "Well if you think about it,

744
00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:24.700
your dog waiting at the door

745
00:35:24.700 --> 00:35:26.050
with his tongue hanging out

746
00:35:26.050 --> 00:35:27.590
kinda waiting for you to come home,

747
00:35:27.590 --> 00:35:29.810
that's a version of hope."

748
00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:32.020
What we've done with our consciousness

749
00:35:32.020 --> 00:35:33.900
and the evolution of our consciousness

750
00:35:33.900 --> 00:35:36.940
is be able to take that hope and go into the distant future

751
00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:39.460
and go to Mars and come,

752
00:35:39.460 --> 00:35:42.360
solve problems far into the distance.

753
00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:43.950
But it is all on a continuum.

754
00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:47.660
And I think that it's almost like she re-grafted us

755
00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:50.290
back into the natural world

756
00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:52.930
and helping us to understand our place.

757
00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:55.330
And I think one of the key things,

758
00:35:55.330 --> 00:35:58.230
if we're gonna solve so many of the problems that we face,

759
00:35:58.230 --> 00:36:01.190
but most especially our environmental crisis,

760
00:36:01.190 --> 00:36:05.460
is that ability to recognize our relationship with the whole

761
00:36:05.460 --> 00:36:06.800
and with the natural world.

762
00:36:06.800 --> 00:36:08.480
And that, as she says,

763
00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:11.670
if we keep pulling threads from the tapestry of life,

764
00:36:11.670 --> 00:36:16.010
it will disintegrate and we will lose what sustains us

765
00:36:16.010 --> 00:36:16.843
and supports us.

766
00:36:16.843 --> 00:36:19.570
Yeah, that's so well put,

767
00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:24.260
the idea that we take for granted this continuum now.

768
00:36:24.260 --> 00:36:25.950
She's like the missing link,

769
00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:29.170
like she's the piece that bridges that gap.

770
00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:30.167
Yeah, and she--

771
00:36:30.167 --> 00:36:33.790
And without her work, how much longer would it have been

772
00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:37.220
before we started to piece that together for ourselves?

773
00:36:37.220 --> 00:36:38.053
It's incredible.

774
00:36:38.053 --> 00:36:38.886
It is amazing.

775
00:36:38.886 --> 00:36:41.250
It's totally epic.

776
00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:46.250
And to have a life where you do that,

777
00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:48.660
just pioneering scientific work

778
00:36:50.672 --> 00:36:53.551
and and then the changes, the zeitgeists,

779
00:36:53.551 --> 00:36:57.100
as Leakey said, "Now we have to,"

780
00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.100
recognizing that animals use tools,

781
00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:01.230
which was one of her discoveries,

782
00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:06.200
when at the time we were called the tool-making ape

783
00:37:06.200 --> 00:37:09.520
that was like only humans use tools,

784
00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:12.680
that ability to shift how we think of ourselves.

785
00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:14.480
And I think what she's doing now,

786
00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:15.970
I mean, one of the interesting things

787
00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:19.050
about the way we talk about "The Book of Hope"

788
