and we're cooperating to compete,

1050
00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:21.450
it's like they're totally interdependent.

1051
00:49:21.450 --> 00:49:22.283
Right.

1052
00:49:22.283 --> 00:49:24.263
So if you take like a forest, for example,

1053
00:49:27.330 --> 00:49:31.080
this idea that perhaps there's a greater intelligence,

1054
00:49:31.080 --> 00:49:32.800
like a hive mind that exists

1055
00:49:32.800 --> 00:49:37.790
amongst the billions of living things within that forest,

1056
00:49:37.790 --> 00:49:40.400
that on some rudimentary level understand

1057
00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:42.050
that their survival is dependent

1058
00:49:42.050 --> 00:49:43.500
upon the survival of the whole.

1059
00:49:43.500 --> 00:49:46.760
So it's not about one tree competing with another tree

1060
00:49:46.760 --> 00:49:49.220
for sunlight or water or et cetera,

1061
00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:51.627
but an understanding that all these trees need to thrive

1062
00:49:51.627 --> 00:49:54.140
and this network has to be healthy.

1063
00:49:54.140 --> 00:49:57.010
And if we can maintain that and sustain that,

1064
00:49:57.010 --> 00:49:58.790
then that's good for everybody.

1065
00:49:58.790 --> 00:49:59.830
It's kind of amazing that,

1066
00:49:59.830 --> 00:50:02.300
so yeah, it's totally, I mean--

1067
00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:03.590
And it works.
It works.

1068
00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:05.220
Without us being involved in it.

1069
00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:06.053
Right, exactly.

1070
00:50:06.053 --> 00:50:08.730
It has that like inherent intelligence.

1071
00:50:08.730 --> 00:50:11.630
And one of the fascinating experience,

1072
00:50:11.630 --> 00:50:15.240
experiments that Suzanne Simard did was she actually found

1073
00:50:15.240 --> 00:50:18.380
that trees exchange carbon with one another.

1074
00:50:18.380 --> 00:50:20.860
So in other words, their kind of version of food,

1075
00:50:20.860 --> 00:50:23.880
based on how much they're shading each other.

1076
00:50:23.880 --> 00:50:26.135
Like they know one needs a little bit more than the other.

1077
00:50:26.135 --> 00:50:26.968
Right, exactly.

1078
00:50:26.968 --> 00:50:29.876
Okay, I'm shading you but I'm gonna give you a little food.

1079
00:50:29.876 --> 00:50:31.193
A kid tied you over there.

1080
00:50:32.220 --> 00:50:36.030
So it's this incredible interdependence and mutuality

1081
00:50:36.030 --> 00:50:38.600
that we've lost sight of.

1082
00:50:38.600 --> 00:50:41.970
And I think kind of this way

1083
00:50:41.970 --> 00:50:44.100
in which like this amazing human intellect

1084
00:50:44.100 --> 00:50:46.660
has been a little too clever for our own good

1085
00:50:46.660 --> 00:50:48.340
and we've separated ourselves

1086
00:50:48.340 --> 00:50:50.650
out from the rest of the natural world

1087
00:50:50.650 --> 00:50:52.650
and from, even from one another

1088
00:50:52.650 --> 00:50:54.850
and recognizing that profound interdependence.

1089
00:50:54.850 --> 00:50:57.610
Yeah, well, part of the problem with the human intellect

1090
00:50:57.610 --> 00:51:00.140
is the hubris that comes with it,

1091
00:51:00.140 --> 00:51:04.660
that we can outengineer this thing and problem solve

1092
00:51:04.660 --> 00:51:07.340
and figure our way through it

1093
00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:10.350
without that level of humility and appreciation.

1094
00:51:10.350 --> 00:51:11.183
Right.

1095
00:51:11.183 --> 00:51:14.570
And I think there's a kind of hyperindividualism that has,

1096
00:51:14.570 --> 00:51:17.340
that we have, especially in the,

1097
00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:20.370
this kind of modern Western culture of,

1098
00:51:20.370 --> 00:51:23.156
that we've lost track of that interdependence

1099
00:51:23.156 --> 00:51:23.989
and that connection.

1100
00:51:23.989 --> 00:51:27.370
And we're not gonna make it if,

1101
00:51:27.370 --> 00:51:31.230
it's like saying, "Okay, it's,"

1102
00:51:31.230 --> 00:51:33.727
we're being taught this lesson in the most profound way,

1103
00:51:33.727 --> 00:51:35.580
"There's one climate."

1104
00:51:35.580 --> 00:51:38.240
If we pollute or somebody else pollutes,

1105
00:51:38.240 --> 00:51:39.300
it doesn't really matter

1106
00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:41.350
because we're all breathing the same air,

1107
00:51:41.350 --> 00:51:43.890
we're all impacted by the same climate.

1108
00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:48.280
But humans are not very well-designed to appreciate that.

1109
00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:52.080
It's amazing how, like I think, it's interesting.

1110
00:51:52.080 --> 00:51:55.090
I mean, when we were hunter-gatherers,

1111
00:51:55.090 --> 00:51:57.630
I think there's this really,

1112
00:51:57.630 --> 00:51:59.920
we had much more of that perspective,

1113
00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:04.920
but we did, we do have this kind of deep competition

1114
00:52:06.920 --> 00:52:11.260
and kind of, potentially Jane also observed the apes

1115
00:52:11.260 --> 00:52:13.550
being genocidal and having a war,

1116
00:52:13.550 --> 00:52:16.130
that was really one of the things that she discovered.

1117
00:52:16.130 --> 00:52:18.547
So we got, there are parts of us that are--

1118
00:52:18.547 --> 00:52:21.050
In us.
Are not going away?

1119
00:52:21.050 --> 00:52:22.420
That it's like we're,

1120
00:52:22.420 --> 00:52:25.060
like I think that any real sustained hope

1121
00:52:25.060 --> 00:52:27.040
has to be based on reality.

1122
00:52:27.040 --> 00:52:28.800
So like to fantasize it,

1123
00:52:28.800 --> 00:52:32.500
somehow we're going to become a different species

1124
00:52:32.500 --> 00:52:35.923
or we're gonna like get rid of our selfishness or our greed.

1125
00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:38.670
That's a fantasy.

1126
00:52:38.670 --> 00:52:40.070
That's fantasizing about the future.

1127
00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:41.990
That's hoping about the future.

1128
00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:45.200
The hope comes from taking what we are

1129
00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:46.590
and the reality of what we are

1130
00:52:46.590 --> 00:52:50.120
and finding out how do we encourage what's best in us.

1131
00:52:50.120 --> 00:52:51.270
Yeah.

1132
00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:54.370
When COVID began and everybody stopped

1133
00:52:54.370 --> 00:52:57.130
and no one was driving and everyone was at home,

1134
00:52:57.130 --> 00:52:59.320
the air seemed to clear pretty quickly,

1135
00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:03.270
as like to this second point of the resilience of nature

1136
00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:04.830
like if we just get out of the way,

1137
00:53:04.830 --> 00:53:06.860
it self-corrects pretty rapidly.

1138
00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:08.450
And my sense is that

1139
00:53:08.450 --> 00:53:10.240
there's a little bit of despair with Jane

1140
00:53:10.240 --> 00:53:12.480
that we couldn't leverage this COVID moment

1141
00:53:12.480 --> 00:53:16.460
to make more substantial changes in the way that we live,

1142
00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:17.950
but it is what it is.

1143
00:53:17.950 --> 00:53:20.460
But there are these amazing examples in the book

1144
00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:22.840
of nature's ability to bounce back

1145
00:53:22.840 --> 00:53:24.370
if we just make some simple changes,

1146
00:53:24.370 --> 00:53:28.930
if we can deploy that human intellect in an appropriate way.

1147
00:53:28.930 --> 00:53:31.810
And I love this story of the cement,

1148
00:53:31.810 --> 00:53:33.940
the guy who owned the cement plant

1149
00:53:33.940 --> 00:53:36.680
and then, I don't know, atoning for that or whatever,

1150
00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:39.040
decides he's gonna restore it to nature

1151
00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:41.950
and it becomes this unbelievable forest preserve,

1152
00:53:41.950 --> 00:53:44.370
and not that long, didn't take that long.

1153
00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:48.410
Well, that was really hopeful to hear that,

1154
00:53:48.410 --> 00:53:53.410
like ecosystems typically bounce back in 10 to 50 years.

1155
00:53:55.420 --> 00:53:57.900
Like if there are marine environments, it's more like 10.

1156
00:53:57.900 --> 00:54:01.300
If there are, depending on the kind of ecosystem it is,

1157
00:54:01.300 --> 00:54:03.900
it can be on the outside of 50 years.

1158
00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:07.650
And I was pushing her, I was like, "How can these,"

1159
00:54:07.650 --> 00:54:10.700
like, "Isn't there a point where nature basically breaks

1160
00:54:10.700 --> 00:54:12.867
and we just destroy it all?"

1161
00:54:14.157 --> 00:54:17.137
And she was saying like,

1162
00:54:17.137 --> 00:54:21.460
"Basically, nature is gonna be here.

1163
00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:23.110
The question is whether we will."

1164
00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:28.370
Like 99.9% of all species

1165
00:54:28.370 --> 00:54:31.200
have gone extinct throughout history.

1166
00:54:31.200 --> 00:54:34.860
So like for us, there is a real chance

1167
00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:35.900
that we could go extinct.

1168
00:54:35.900 --> 00:54:39.010
And the question is, are we going to,

1169
00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:41.690
we can't evolve physically anymore,

1170
00:54:41.690 --> 00:54:44.690
we just don't, we're not gonna do that at any timescale

1171
00:54:44.690 --> 00:54:48.200
that is fast enough, so can we evolve culturally,

1172
00:54:48.200 --> 00:54:53.200
fast enough so that we can do what is necessary

1173
00:54:53.210 --> 00:54:55.970
in order to stick around and earn our place?

1174
00:54:55.970 --> 00:54:58.980
I mean, there's this fascinating woman I spoke to

1175
00:54:58.980 --> 00:55:01.820
named Janine Benyus who does this work on biomimicry,

1176
00:55:01.820 --> 00:55:03.207
and she said,

1177
00:55:03.207 --> 00:55:06.510
"The difference between being an invasive species

1178
00:55:06.510 --> 00:55:07.970
and a welcome species,"

1179
00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:11.467
like a naturalized species in any ecosystem,

1180
00:55:11.467 --> 00:55:15.030
"is an invasive species takes more than they give,

1181
00:55:15.030 --> 00:55:17.520
a welcome species gives more to the ecosystem

1182
00:55:17.520 --> 00:55:18.410
than they take."

1183
00:55:18.410 --> 00:55:22.200
And so we've been taking and taking and taking

1184
00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:24.470
and been this invasive species.

1185
00:55:24.470 --> 00:55:26.700
So now the work

1186
00:55:26.700 --> 00:55:31.390
is can we actually reimagine our culture

1187
00:55:31.390 --> 00:55:34.940
and our society and our way of life

1188
00:55:34.940 --> 00:55:36.920
so that actually gives more than we take?

1189
00:55:36.920 --> 00:55:40.530
And having just been at the TED Climate Countdown

1190
00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:42.110
with one of our other authors,

1191
00:55:42.110 --> 00:55:44.760
Christiana Figueres who did the Paris Climate Treaty,

1192
00:55:44.760 --> 00:55:48.890
it was so inspiring to see how we are coming up

1193
00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:51.910
with so many of these technological solutions,

1194
00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:55.480
but also just recognizing this,

1195
00:55:55.480 --> 00:56:00.020
that we don't have to be this kind of extractive society;

1196
00:56:00.020 --> 00:56:02.870
we can actually give back to the environment

1197
00:56:02.870 --> 00:56:07.330
and create technologies and ways of living

1198
00:56:07.330 --> 00:56:09.530
that actually give as much as we take.

1199
00:56:09.530 --> 00:56:14.530
And fundamentally, dealing with the climate crisis we're in

1200
00:56:15.610 --> 00:56:17.100
is not waiting around

1201
00:56:17.100 --> 00:56:21.140
for some technological bright miracle to happen.

1202
00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:22.910
It's really, I mean, they were quantifying it

1203
00:56:22.910 --> 00:56:25.810
as the trillions of dollars we need to be investing

1204
00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:29.020
over the next 10 years to transform our infrastructure

1205
00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:32.670
and our way of life, and we know what the technologies are,

1206
00:56:32.670 --> 00:56:35.320
we just need the political will to do it.

1207
00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:37.977
Yeah, the political will is problematic.

1208
00:56:37.977 --> 00:56:39.280
(Doug laughs)

1209
00:56:39.280 --> 00:56:41.080
Then we can get hopeless about it.

1210
00:56:42.052 --> 00:56:42.885
Our society, yeah.

1211
00:56:42.885 --> 00:56:44.730
Yeah, because we need it on both ends

1212
00:56:44.730 --> 00:56:49.730
and Jane talks about how we've been told to think globally

1213
00:56:50.510 --> 00:56:53.210
and act locally and how that actually leads to depression.

1214
00:56:53.210 --> 00:56:54.580
Because when you think globally,

1215
00:56:54.580 --> 00:56:57.340
it's too overwhelming for you to do anything.

1216
00:56:57.340 --> 00:56:59.430
So what's the alternative?

1217
00:56:59.430 --> 00:57:02.010
Well, we can like not use plastic straws.

1218
00:57:02.010 --> 00:57:04.700
And I guess there's some level of agency

1219
00:57:04.700 --> 00:57:05.600
that we feel with that.

1220
00:57:05.600 --> 00:57:08.170
We can adopt a plant-based diet, we can recycle,

1221
00:57:08.170 --> 00:57:11.900
we can compost, we can gauge our food weight.

1222
00:57:11.900 --> 00:57:15.140
There are plenty of thing, we can get renewable energy.

1223
00:57:15.140 --> 00:57:15.973
So all these things,

1224
00:57:15.973 --> 00:57:18.700
that there are certain consumer things that we can do

1225
00:57:18.700 --> 00:57:21.600
and that basically allows us

1226
00:57:21.600 --> 00:57:24.370
to feel more emotionally invested

1227
00:57:24.370 --> 00:57:26.380
and develops that sense of agency,

1228
00:57:26.380 --> 00:57:30.180
like at least we're taking the small actions that we can

1229
00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:32.997
every single day, but on some level there is a sense like,

1230
00:57:32.997 --> 00:57:34.600
"Yeah, that's not gonna cut it.

1231
00:57:34.600 --> 00:57:36.550
Like we need it on the other end, too.

1232
00:57:36.550 --> 00:57:38.970
We need it from the highest levels down."

1233
00:57:38.970 --> 00:57:41.320
And I'm glad to hear

1234
00:57:41.320 --> 00:57:45.180
that you came out of the TED conference somewhat inspired.

1235
00:57:45.180 --> 00:57:47.710
And here we are on the cusp of COP26,

1236
00:57:47.710 --> 00:57:49.650
like there's a lot of speculation

1237
00:57:49.650 --> 00:57:52.110
about whether we can get our shit together

1238
00:57:52.110 --> 00:57:56.320
and deploy the level of political

1239
00:57:56.320 --> 00:58:00.970
and financial resources that are gonna be required

1240
00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:03.690
to tackle this in a really meaningful way.

1241
00:58:03.690 --> 00:58:06.100
The future was never one without a battle.

1242
00:58:06.100 --> 00:58:10.680
And I think we are in a battle for the soul

1243
00:58:10.680 --> 00:58:13.460
and future of humanity.

1244
00:58:13.460 --> 00:58:16.750
And it's not gonna be easy.

1245
00:58:16.750 --> 00:58:19.050
And whether we succeed at COP or,

1246
00:58:19.050 --> 00:58:21.710
like there were a lot of COPs before Paris

1247
00:58:21.710 --> 00:58:26.010
that Christiana was first to get the treaty,

1248
00:58:26.010 --> 00:58:30.020
that she got was quite miraculous, got 190 countries.

1249
00:58:30.020 --> 00:58:32.090
But what's amazing about,

1250
00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.520
and it's not happening fast enough.

1251
00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:36.790
There are all sorts of parties

1252
00:58:36.790 --> 00:58:39.513
that are not pulling their weight, ourselves included.

1253
00:58:40.810 --> 00:58:43.320
And yet, like at that conference,

1254
00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:45.290
I was talking with somebody

1255
00:58:45.290 --> 00:58:48.260
who represents the top 10 oil and gas companies

1256
00:58:48.260 --> 00:58:50.580
and who are obviously some of the villains

1257
00:58:50.580 --> 00:58:53.180
in this story of destruction of our planet,

1258
00:58:53.180 --> 00:58:58.180
and they are committed to net-zero by 2050.

1259
00:58:58.360 --> 00:59:01.650
They've basically recognized the writing on the wall

1260
00:59:01.650 --> 00:59:04.730
that there is no,

1261
00:59:04.730 --> 00:59:07.820
like they have to become renewable energy companies.

1262
00:59:07.820 --> 00:59:12.820
I mean, the fact that solar and wind have,

1263
00:59:13.200 --> 00:59:16.090
prices have declined as dramatically as they have,

1264
00:59:16.090 --> 00:59:19.510
that a third of all energy right now is already renewable.

1265
00:59:19.510 --> 00:59:21.900
I mean, these are things that,

1266
00:59:21.900 --> 00:59:25.960
like you could've been, we could've been on this program

1267
00:59:25.960 --> 00:59:27.012
a couple of years to say,

1268
00:59:27.012 --> 00:59:29.110
"Oh, it's never gonna happen," right?

1269
00:59:29.110 --> 00:59:33.460
And so I think what, it was really interesting

1270
00:59:34.611 --> 00:59:38.780
to hear Jane's comment of having lived through World War II

1271
00:59:38.780 --> 00:59:41.990
and having lived through democracy

1272
00:59:41.990 --> 00:59:46.200
on the precipice of extinction in the face of fascism

1273
00:59:46.200 --> 00:59:51.200
and in the face of Nazi Germany and the bombings.

1274
00:59:53.020 --> 00:59:56.880
History does not happen in a textbook;

1275
00:59:56.880 --> 01:00:00.870
it happens because individuals make decisions

1276
01:00:00.870 --> 01:00:02.170
that change that history.

1277
01:00:02.170 --> 01:00:07.170
And it's so easy to think about history and society

1278
01:00:07.740 --> 01:00:10.640
as being these big political bodies

1279
01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:13.630
or these big corporations, and who are we?

1280
01:00:13.630 --> 01:00:16.900
But as you said, our consumer choices

1281
01:00:16.900 --> 01:00:20.983
drive a lot of those corporations to change.

1282
01:00:22.330 --> 01:00:24.750
Fortunately, in democratic societies,

1283
01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:29.177
we are the ones who do elect our political leaders

1284
01:00:30.360 --> 01:00:34.483
and determine what policies we are going to follow.

1285
01:00:35.570 --> 01:00:37.980
So a lot of it, I think, is,

1286
01:00:37.980 --> 01:00:39.520
I think what Jane would say is,

1287
01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:40.600
I say, I think you're right,

1288
01:00:40.600 --> 01:00:42.483
and what Jane would say is,

1289
01:00:43.877 --> 01:00:45.680
"It's not about paper or plastic,

1290
01:00:45.680 --> 01:00:49.850
it's not about like that decision is gonna change

1291
01:00:49.850 --> 01:00:51.210
the course of history;

1292
01:00:51.210 --> 01:00:54.490
but it is about the overall carbon choices

1293
01:00:54.490 --> 01:00:56.320
that we each make in our own lives

1294
01:00:56.320 --> 01:00:59.810
and it is also about our political will."

1295
01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:03.420
I mean, look at a school girl in Sweden

1296
01:01:03.420 --> 01:01:07.470
named Greta Thunberg, who was able to unleash

1297
01:01:07.470 --> 01:01:08.750
an entire youth movement

1298
01:01:08.750 --> 01:01:10.540
because she was willing to miss school

1299
01:01:10.540 --> 01:01:12.640
and sit in front of the parliament, right?

1300
01:01:14.880 --> 01:01:17.740
You could've said, "Who is she,"

1301
01:01:17.740 --> 01:01:19.690
but look at the impact that she's had.

1302
01:01:19.690 --> 01:01:21.300
Yeah, it's unbelievable.

1303
01:01:21.300 --> 01:01:25.590
I've thought a lot about the juxtaposition of Jane and Greta

1304
01:01:25.590 --> 01:01:28.770
because they're sort of analogs to each other,

1305
01:01:28.770 --> 01:01:31.480
like on some level you could make the argument

1306
01:01:31.480 --> 01:01:35.693
that Greta is the sort of successor

1307
01:01:37.610 --> 01:01:42.610
to a certain strain of advocacy that Jane helped initiate,

1308
01:01:42.650 --> 01:01:43.810
and yet they're very different.

1309
01:01:43.810 --> 01:01:46.820
Like generationally, they have very different sensibilities

1310
01:01:46.820 --> 01:01:48.120
in how they advocate.

1311
01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:49.757
And you asked Jane point blank like,

1312
01:01:49.757 --> 01:01:50.790
"What do you think of Greta,"

1313
01:01:50.790 --> 01:01:51.623
right?
Yeah.

1314
01:01:51.623 --> 01:01:53.700
And she was very politic in her answer.

1315
01:01:53.700 --> 01:01:58.700
I suspect that her position is like, "That works for Greta,"

1316
01:01:59.670 --> 01:02:04.280
like Jane is much more empathetic, she's much more gentle,

1317
01:02:04.280 --> 01:02:07.890
she's very direct and clear in her messaging,

1318
01:02:07.890 --> 01:02:11.690
but she doesn't come, she doesn't come at it

1319
01:02:11.690 --> 01:02:13.870
as hard as Greta does.

1320
01:02:13.870 --> 01:02:15.420
And you need every, you need both--

1321
01:02:15.420 --> 01:02:16.390
Exactly.
You need all of it.

1322
01:02:16.390 --> 01:02:19.570
It's all great, but it's interesting how they differ.

1323
01:02:19.570 --> 01:02:20.570
Yeah, it is really interesting.

1324
01:02:20.570 --> 01:02:23.777
I loved it when I asked her like,

1325
01:02:23.777 --> 01:02:26.090
"What do you say to somebody like Greta,

1326
01:02:26.090 --> 01:02:28.890
who says, 'Don't give us your hope; give us your fear,'"

1327
01:02:31.570 --> 01:02:33.260
realize that your house is on fire

1328
01:02:33.260 --> 01:02:36.360
and act like your house is on fire, right?

1329
01:02:36.360 --> 01:02:41.290
And Jane's response that we need fear, anger, and hope,

1330
01:02:41.290 --> 01:02:43.230
we need all of 'em.

1331
01:02:43.230 --> 01:02:46.220
It's not fear alone, it's not anger alone,

1332
01:02:46.220 --> 01:02:47.590
it's not hope alone.

1333
01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:51.140
We need all of these responses

1334
01:02:51.140 --> 01:02:53.310
to the crisis that we're facing.

1335
01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:56.000
And I do think, to your point

1336
01:02:56.000 --> 01:02:57.900
about they're being really different,

1337
01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:01.423
I think that Jane is more,

1338
01:03:02.780 --> 01:03:04.560
I mean, one of the amazing things that Jane did

1339
01:03:04.560 --> 01:03:09.560
was to get animal testing to be outlawed on chimpanzees,

1340
01:03:10.630 --> 01:03:13.460
where we were doing all these experiments on animals

1341
01:03:13.460 --> 01:03:16.170
and she's been really active in animal rights issues,

1342
01:03:16.170 --> 01:03:18.780
and was a huge activist.

1343
01:03:18.780 --> 01:03:22.120
Her experience was that she got a lot of those changes

1344
01:03:22.120 --> 01:03:24.280
by changing, telling stories

1345
01:03:24.280 --> 01:03:25.700
and changing the hearts and minds

1346
01:03:25.700 --> 01:03:27.950
of the people who are making those decisions

1347
01:03:27.950 --> 01:03:30.540
and I think that that's a really powerful

1348
01:03:30.540 --> 01:03:32.220
and important way to do it.

1349
01:03:32.220 --> 01:03:34.640
I also think that there's a role, I mean, personally,

1350
01:03:34.640 --> 01:03:37.530
I think there's a role for demonstration

1351
01:03:37.530 --> 01:03:38.910
and civil disobedience

1352
01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:43.910
and a kind of harder edged, angrier activism that,

1353
01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:46.220
and I think young people recognize

1354
01:03:46.220 --> 01:03:48.120
that it's their future that's at stake

1355
01:03:48.120 --> 01:03:50.130
and that we need to change.

1356
01:03:50.130 --> 01:03:53.220
Yeah, it's a tension between that absolutism.

1357
01:03:53.220 --> 01:03:55.117
Like Greta would probably say like,

1358
01:03:55.117 --> 01:03:57.380
"Well, Jane's been talking about this since the '80s.

1359
01:03:57.380 --> 01:03:59.810
Like, I don't wanna have to talk about it for 50 years

1360
01:03:59.810 --> 01:04:01.273
before something changes."

1361
01:04:01.273 --> 01:04:04.220
Sort of the impetuousness of youth

1362
01:04:04.220 --> 01:04:06.840
versus the kind of coalition building.

1363
01:04:06.840 --> 01:04:08.010
Like, I think storytelling

1364
01:04:08.010 --> 01:04:11.080
really is the best way to win hearts and minds,

1365
01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:14.370
but there's a patience required with that as well.

1366
01:04:14.370 --> 01:04:17.700
And there's this idea of being in partnership

1367
01:04:17.700 --> 01:04:20.640
or cooperation with some of the bad actors

1368
01:04:20.640 --> 01:04:24.230
to help them develop a greater awareness

1369
01:04:24.230 --> 01:04:27.650
of what's actually going on and kind of shift the incentives

1370
01:04:27.650 --> 01:04:30.700
to nudge them into the right direction.

1371
01:04:30.700 --> 01:04:34.080
But those two things working in concert with each other,

1372
01:04:34.080 --> 01:04:35.350
sometimes in opposition

1373
01:04:35.350 --> 01:04:37.313
but ultimately with the same goal in mind,

1374
01:04:37.313 --> 01:04:41.150
I think are, become powerful like dual drivers.

1375
01:04:41.150 --> 01:04:41.983
Absolutely.

1376
01:04:41.983 --> 01:04:42.816
I think you need it all.

1377
01:04:42.816 --> 01:04:46.430
And you need, both are all of the options.

1378
01:04:46.430 --> 01:04:49.410
You need to be driving internal change

1379
01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:51.100
and you need to be driving external change.

1380
01:04:51.100 --> 01:04:52.500
I mean, one of the things,

1381
01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:55.270
the stories that we tell in the book was from Christiana

1382
01:04:55.270 --> 01:04:56.830
of the kinda CEO of Shell,

1383
01:04:56.830 --> 01:04:58.320
who was at this TED climate meeting,

1384
01:04:58.320 --> 01:05:00.667
basically saying that his daughter had come to him and said,

1385
01:05:00.667 --> 01:05:02.630
"Are you destroying the planet?

1386
01:05:02.630 --> 01:05:05.030
Because that's what I'm hearing in school,

1387
01:05:05.030 --> 01:05:08.267
that you're ruining the planet and Shell oil."

1388
01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:13.370
His change of heart and mind,

1389
01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:15.360
you can ask whether it's greenwashing

1390
01:05:15.360 --> 01:05:18.160
or how sincere it is or how real it is,

1391
01:05:18.160 --> 01:05:21.057
but his desire as a father to, as he says,

1392
01:05:21.057 --> 01:05:24.707
"Get this done for you and your generation,"

1393
01:05:26.890 --> 01:05:29.740
there's that kind of emotional shift and change.

1394
01:05:29.740 --> 01:05:33.150
But we also need the demonstrations and the political will

1395
01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:37.670
to be taxing carbon in a way that makes it,

1396
01:05:37.670 --> 01:05:40.270
makes sense financially and as a business

1397
01:05:40.270 --> 01:05:45.270
and absolutely legally necessary to make the shifts

1398
01:05:46.640 --> 01:05:49.710
because we can't depend on everybody's hearts and minds

1399
01:05:49.710 --> 01:05:51.200
changing in time.

1400
01:05:51.200 --> 01:05:53.981
Are you going to COP26

1401
01:05:53.981 --> 01:05:56.440
now that you have this, you have a client and author

1402
01:05:56.440 --> 01:05:58.700
who's like immersed in that?

1403
01:05:58.700 --> 01:06:02.010
Yeah, so I decided that I wasn't,

1404
01:06:02.010 --> 01:06:03.720
this is more of a policy forum

1405
01:06:03.720 --> 01:06:08.370
and the TED climate meeting was more of a kind of culture

1406
01:06:08.370 --> 01:06:10.130
and thought leadership forum,

1407
01:06:10.130 --> 01:06:12.880
that that was a place I could be most useful.

1408
01:06:12.880 --> 01:06:16.490
So there's still a possibility that I may go,

1409
01:06:16.490 --> 01:06:21.080
but it's, I think, again, to wanna use the carbon

1410
01:06:21.080 --> 01:06:24.063
to get there for only if I'm needed and useful.

1411
01:06:26.050 --> 01:06:27.420
Sorry to interrupt the flow,

1412
01:06:27.420 --> 01:06:29.230
we'll be right back with more awesome.

1413
