01:07:51.210 --> 01:07:53.183 line:15%
All right, back to the pod.

1444
01:07:54.230 --> 01:07:56.020
I mean, the other pillar here

1445
01:07:56.020 --> 01:07:57.570
is the power of young people, right?

1446
01:07:57.570 --> 01:07:59.020
And this is another thing

1447
01:07:59.020 --> 01:08:01.010
that like Jane was way ahead of the curve on.

1448
01:08:01.010 --> 01:08:03.460
She starts this roots and shoots thing

1449
01:08:03.460 --> 01:08:05.250
that is empowering all these young people

1450
01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:06.900
and engendering that level of hope

1451
01:08:06.900 --> 01:08:09.120
by connecting them with natural environments

1452
01:08:09.120 --> 01:08:12.112
and giving them tools and resources

1453
01:08:12.112 --> 01:08:14.600
to actually, like in a very tactile way,

1454
01:08:14.600 --> 01:08:19.310
like participate in creating the change.

1455
01:08:19.310 --> 01:08:20.143
Yeah.

1456
01:08:20.143 --> 01:08:23.320
I mean, it's often called the Jane generation.

1457
01:08:23.320 --> 01:08:25.430
She's like, in 68 countries,

1458
01:08:25.430 --> 01:08:27.220
she's been having these programs

1459
01:08:27.220 --> 01:08:29.890
which have been helping activate

1460
01:08:29.890 --> 01:08:32.830
and get kids into activism

1461
01:08:32.830 --> 01:08:35.920
and into kinda caring about their environments

1462
01:08:35.920 --> 01:08:37.840
and their communities.

1463
01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:40.130
So I think it's a powerful example

1464
01:08:40.130 --> 01:08:45.100
of the ways in which you can intervene in the culture

1465
01:08:45.100 --> 01:08:48.820
and actually change hearts and minds at a very young age.

1466
01:08:48.820 --> 01:08:50.770
I asked her, I was pretty skeptical,

1467
01:08:50.770 --> 01:08:52.510
because I was like, "How can you say

1468
01:08:52.510 --> 01:08:55.230
the power of young people is gonna save us

1469
01:08:55.230 --> 01:08:57.930
or is gonna, is this reason for hope,

1470
01:08:57.930 --> 01:09:00.450
where we haven't been able to get,

1471
01:09:00.450 --> 01:09:04.241
other previous generations haven't been able to get it done,

1472
01:09:04.241 --> 01:09:07.520
and what's to say that they're gonna be different?"

1473
01:09:07.520 --> 01:09:08.353
And she said,

1474
01:09:08.353 --> 01:09:11.320
"Well, I think in terms of the environment in particular,"

1475
01:09:11.320 --> 01:09:14.637
but I think in terms of social awareness more generally,

1476
01:09:14.637 --> 01:09:15.720
"they are aware."

1477
01:09:15.720 --> 01:09:18.620
And I mean, I think this is one of the amazing things

1478
01:09:18.620 --> 01:09:19.520
about our species.

1479
01:09:20.529 --> 01:09:21.362
We talked about this a little,

1480
01:09:21.362 --> 01:09:26.362
but we have this kind of prolonged childhood

1481
01:09:26.780 --> 01:09:31.780
in which we are, we create this whole kind of nurturance

1482
01:09:33.400 --> 01:09:35.710
that transforms and educates

1483
01:09:35.710 --> 01:09:38.043
and changes the next generation.

1484
01:09:39.300 --> 01:09:40.570
A lot of species obviously,

1485
01:09:40.570 --> 01:09:42.810
they're basically pre-programmed.

1486
01:09:42.810 --> 01:09:46.160
They come out, every generation does the same thing

1487
01:09:46.160 --> 01:09:47.720
as the last generation.

1488
01:09:47.720 --> 01:09:49.270
We have this capacity,

1489
01:09:49.270 --> 01:09:52.240
we call it education, we call it parenthood,

1490
01:09:52.240 --> 01:09:55.730
to actually change and transform the next generation

1491
01:09:55.730 --> 01:10:00.730
to evolve our species in this really amazing way

1492
01:10:01.010 --> 01:10:02.570
from generation to generation.

1493
01:10:02.570 --> 01:10:04.590
And if you think about it, I mean, this is,

1494
01:10:04.590 --> 01:10:06.543
also back to the work with the Dalai Lama and Desmond Tutu

1495
01:10:06.543 --> 01:10:09.430
because we talked a lot about hope and despair

1496
01:10:09.430 --> 01:10:11.590
and the state of the world,

1497
01:10:11.590 --> 01:10:14.227
and if you look at the news, as they said,

1498
01:10:14.227 --> 01:10:18.240
"All you see on this planet is the doom and gloom."

1499
01:10:18.240 --> 01:10:22.170
But it's news because it's actually unusual,

1500
01:10:22.170 --> 01:10:23.830
it's not the vast majority

1501
01:10:23.830 --> 01:10:25.530
of what's happening on this planet.

1502
01:10:25.530 --> 01:10:30.490
And so if you look at the reality of most of human life,

1503
01:10:30.490 --> 01:10:33.290
there's an enormous amount of compassion and kindness

1504
01:10:33.290 --> 01:10:37.650
and caring that takes place every day that we're doing.

1505
01:10:37.650 --> 01:10:39.470
And if you think about, the other thing they said,

1506
01:10:39.470 --> 01:10:41.893
if you take this long view and you see like,

1507
01:10:43.052 --> 01:10:45.562
"What was our society like 100 years ago?"

1508
01:10:45.562 --> 01:10:50.255
Like, women couldn't vote just over a century ago.

1509
01:10:50.255 --> 01:10:51.610
Yeah, I mean, it was like five seconds ago.

1510
01:10:51.610 --> 01:10:52.950
Yeah, five seconds ago, right?

1511
01:10:52.950 --> 01:10:56.260
I mean, like the transformation, we used to think,

1512
01:10:56.260 --> 01:11:01.120
we thought that slavery was like morally justifiable,

1513
01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:06.031
like less than 200 years ago, right?

1514
01:11:06.031 --> 01:11:10.060
I mean, we have been evolving our culture

1515
01:11:10.060 --> 01:11:13.610
in these phenomenal and dramatic ways

1516
01:11:13.610 --> 01:11:15.800
and it's so easy to forget that

1517
01:11:15.800 --> 01:11:18.890
and to get caught up with the quarterly cycle

1518
01:11:18.890 --> 01:11:23.890
or the political cycle and to say, "Oh God, we're doomed."

1519
01:11:24.180 --> 01:11:29.180
But if you think about it as this much larger evolution

1520
01:11:29.280 --> 01:11:33.130
and process of transformation, then it's really a question,

1521
01:11:33.130 --> 01:11:36.300
it's not like whether we're gonna deal with the crises

1522
01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:40.530
because we are gonna have to or we're gonna go extinct.

1523
01:11:40.530 --> 01:11:43.610
It's really a question of how skillfully we do that,

1524
01:11:43.610 --> 01:11:46.330
how fast we do that, how much human suffering,

1525
01:11:46.330 --> 01:11:50.250
and suffering of humans and the rest of the natural world

1526
01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:53.480
we're going to need in order to get where we need to go.

1527
01:11:53.480 --> 01:11:54.330
Yeah.

1528
01:11:54.330 --> 01:11:56.580
I don't know that we've forgotten

1529
01:11:57.500 --> 01:11:58.930
how much we have progressed

1530
01:11:58.930 --> 01:12:02.440
as much as we're literally brainwashed,

1531
01:12:02.440 --> 01:12:05.840
by dent of our media landscape,

1532
01:12:05.840 --> 01:12:08.740
into believing that everything is terrible all the time.

1533
01:12:08.740 --> 01:12:09.573
And this is something

1534
01:12:09.573 --> 01:12:12.270
that like Steven Pinker talks about all the time.

1535
01:12:12.270 --> 01:12:16.740
We're actually way better off than most of us recognize.

1536
01:12:16.740 --> 01:12:19.430
And if you really look at just a few years ago,

1537
01:12:19.430 --> 01:12:21.220
decades ago, hundreds of years ago,

1538
01:12:21.220 --> 01:12:25.050
I mean, it's just, it's statistically by every metric

1539
01:12:25.050 --> 01:12:27.320
like we're in a much better place.

1540
01:12:27.320 --> 01:12:31.237
And it's almost as if we need a 24-hour network

1541
01:12:33.040 --> 01:12:35.660
that is just sharing the uplifting stories

1542
01:12:35.660 --> 01:12:39.840
or telling the tale of like how we're progressing

1543
01:12:39.840 --> 01:12:41.870
in meaningful, positive ways,

1544
01:12:41.870 --> 01:12:45.690
that could be a germinate of that kind of hope.

1545
01:12:45.690 --> 01:12:47.230
But there's nothing like that

1546
01:12:47.230 --> 01:12:49.750
and perhaps there's no economic,

1547
01:12:49.750 --> 01:12:52.710
a viable economic model to create something like that.

1548
01:12:52.710 --> 01:12:55.390
But imagine the impact of something like that.

1549
01:12:55.390 --> 01:12:59.100
And we all celebrate lives like Jane

1550
01:12:59.100 --> 01:13:03.180
and stories that make us feel uplifted

1551
01:13:03.180 --> 01:13:04.990
and yet when push comes to shove,

1552
01:13:04.990 --> 01:13:08.790
we click the channel and we indulge ourselves

1553
01:13:08.790 --> 01:13:11.410
in whatever disasters are happening around the world

1554
01:13:11.410 --> 01:13:14.970
like every single day or scrolling through our Twitter feed.

1555
01:13:14.970 --> 01:13:18.680
Yeah, I think there's something that the neuroscientists

1556
01:13:18.680 --> 01:13:20.860
call the negative bias of the brain,

1557
01:13:20.860 --> 01:13:24.700
which is we evolved to look for the problem,

1558
01:13:24.700 --> 01:13:26.900
we evolved to look for what's gonna kill us.

1559
01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:30.520
To look for the threat, the danger.

1560
01:13:30.520 --> 01:13:33.710
And so it's not surprising

1561
01:13:33.710 --> 01:13:37.870
that we're kind of glued to the car wreck

1562
01:13:37.870 --> 01:13:40.160
and to see--
Disaster porn.

1563
01:13:40.160 --> 01:13:41.825
Disaster porn.

1564
01:13:41.825 --> 01:13:46.825
And that is really fascinating to the human imagination

1565
01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:47.973
and the human mind.

1566
01:13:47.973 --> 01:13:49.970
And again, that's not going away either.

1567
01:13:49.970 --> 01:13:53.280
And I think that the,

1568
01:13:53.280 --> 01:13:57.190
and yes, I think the media has become this kind of,

1569
01:13:57.190 --> 01:14:01.540
like if you think about pre-media culture,

1570
01:14:01.540 --> 01:14:06.540
like 100 years ago people were not saturated

1571
01:14:07.050 --> 01:14:10.230
with this kind of, the horrible things

1572
01:14:10.230 --> 01:14:12.360
that are happening everywhere in the world, right?

1573
01:14:12.360 --> 01:14:14.680
That was kinda like in your neighborhood.

1574
01:14:14.680 --> 01:14:16.223
If shit going down, we're not.

1575
01:14:19.296 --> 01:14:23.480
You just didn't have that same kind of global perspective

1576
01:14:23.480 --> 01:14:26.410
and you weren't being, as you said,

1577
01:14:26.410 --> 01:14:31.223
saturated by disaster porn or misery porn or outrage.

1578
01:14:32.324 --> 01:14:34.760
And I think this is an example of

1579
01:14:34.760 --> 01:14:37.700
do our algorithms encourage outrage

1580
01:14:37.700 --> 01:14:39.490
or do they encourage the facts

1581
01:14:39.490 --> 01:14:42.520
and a more balanced view of life?

1582
01:14:42.520 --> 01:14:44.960
Well, I think we've answered that question.

1583
01:14:44.960 --> 01:14:46.010
Certainly the algorithms,

1584
01:14:46.010 --> 01:14:49.000
as they are programmed now, do, right?

1585
01:14:49.000 --> 01:14:50.540
Kind of enhanced the outrage

1586
01:14:50.540 --> 01:14:53.220
and the despair and the depression.

1587
01:14:53.220 --> 01:14:54.053
I mean, it's interesting.

1588
01:14:54.053 --> 01:14:55.730
We work with a researcher who studies

1589
01:14:55.730 --> 01:14:57.980
the impact of social media

1590
01:14:57.980 --> 01:15:01.180
on people's happiness and well-being and they found that--

1591
01:15:01.180 --> 01:15:02.013
BJ?

1592
01:15:02.013 --> 01:15:03.540
No, actually we work with BJ.

1593
01:15:03.540 --> 01:15:05.435
Yes, this is actually--

1594
01:15:05.435 --> 01:15:07.670
He's like patient zero for that, right?

1595
01:15:07.670 --> 01:15:10.970
He taught the class that spawned all these technologists

1596
01:15:10.970 --> 01:15:12.772
that went out and created algorithms.

1597
01:15:12.772 --> 01:15:13.830
Yes, exactly.

1598
01:15:13.830 --> 01:15:15.090
That's the problem with technology.

1599
01:15:15.090 --> 01:15:17.150
And in science, you can use it for good

1600
01:15:17.150 --> 01:15:18.500
or you can use it for evil.

1601
01:15:20.450 --> 01:15:21.830
No, this is actually Ethan Kross.

1602
01:15:21.830 --> 01:15:24.140
And one of the things he discovered was

1603
01:15:24.140 --> 01:15:27.010
that when people passively consume social media,

1604
01:15:27.010 --> 01:15:28.730
they're just like scrolling through their feed,

1605
01:15:28.730 --> 01:15:31.510
yeah, they're more miserable and unhappy

1606
01:15:31.510 --> 01:15:32.890
for a whole variety of reasons

1607
01:15:32.890 --> 01:15:34.530
that we don't need to go into.

1608
01:15:34.530 --> 01:15:37.750
But if they're more engaged in it

1609
01:15:37.750 --> 01:15:40.100
and using it for that social support,

1610
01:15:40.100 --> 01:15:43.113
for that hope contagion, for that,

1611
01:15:44.270 --> 01:15:46.390
that hope is a social gift,

1612
01:15:46.390 --> 01:15:48.730
and getting support and giving support,

1613
01:15:48.730 --> 01:15:51.310
that's where it can be actually really useful.

1614
01:15:51.310 --> 01:15:52.960
Yeah, yeah, that's interesting.

1615
01:15:53.980 --> 01:15:56.700
One of the ways that I try to communicate to my kids

1616
01:15:56.700 --> 01:15:58.890
around this stuff is to think of it

1617
01:15:58.890 --> 01:16:01.270
in terms of consumption versus creation.

1618
01:16:01.270 --> 01:16:03.990
Like, are you using this to create something?

1619
01:16:03.990 --> 01:16:06.110
Are you using it to have healthy communication

1620
01:16:06.110 --> 01:16:09.080
or are you just mindlessly consuming it?

1621
01:16:09.080 --> 01:16:11.350
And if you're using it for creative purposes,

1622
01:16:11.350 --> 01:16:14.484
then it becomes an incredibly powerful

1623
01:16:14.484 --> 01:16:16.630
and often uplifting tool.

1624
01:16:16.630 --> 01:16:17.610
Right.

1625
01:16:17.610 --> 01:16:20.640
I mean, in some ways one really fascinating way

1626
01:16:20.640 --> 01:16:24.020
to think of social media is we've just basically wired up

1627
01:16:24.020 --> 01:16:28.220
all of our brains together into a global mind.

1628
01:16:28.220 --> 01:16:32.750
And now the question is like, so the human mind is,

1629
01:16:32.750 --> 01:16:34.680
there's the amazing human intellect,

1630
01:16:34.680 --> 01:16:36.580
but there are all sorts of other that it'll,

1631
01:16:36.580 --> 01:16:38.470
challenges of the human mind too.

1632
01:16:38.470 --> 01:16:39.985
Little dark corners.

1633
01:16:39.985 --> 01:16:41.340
(all laugh)

1634
01:16:41.340 --> 01:16:45.730
We ruminate a lot, we dwell, we can be really cruel,

1635
01:16:45.730 --> 01:16:49.230
like all sorts of things that we can do with our mind.

1636
01:16:49.230 --> 01:16:50.720
And so the question is,

1637
01:16:50.720 --> 01:16:54.610
now we've, for the first time, we have this global mind

1638
01:16:54.610 --> 01:16:59.610
and how do we take the realities of the human mind,

1639
01:17:00.930 --> 01:17:02.510
the individual human mind,

1640
01:17:02.510 --> 01:17:07.510
and how do we wire up a global mind that actually helps us

1641
01:17:07.850 --> 01:17:09.870
and encourages us, as Jane was saying,

1642
01:17:09.870 --> 01:17:12.330
like an environment that inclines us

1643
01:17:12.330 --> 01:17:15.660
toward who we need to be and how we need to be

1644
01:17:15.660 --> 01:17:19.230
in order to evolve and change and sustain ourselves.

1645
01:17:19.230 --> 01:17:21.640
Right, if you could take that global hive mind

1646
01:17:21.640 --> 01:17:25.130
and direct it towards positive change,

1647
01:17:25.130 --> 01:17:28.110
there can be no power, more powerful force.

1648
01:17:28.110 --> 01:17:29.100
Exactly, I mean, you said--

1649
01:17:29.100 --> 01:17:31.070
And therein lies the hope.

1650
01:17:31.070 --> 01:17:35.010
Well, you saw it in the Arab Spring,

1651
01:17:35.010 --> 01:17:37.460
which obviously had its challenges

1652
01:17:37.460 --> 01:17:40.920
and didn't all go the way we wanted it to go.

1653
01:17:40.920 --> 01:17:44.670
But social media played a really important role in that.

1654
01:17:44.670 --> 01:17:48.260
And I think, and also in societies

1655
01:17:48.260 --> 01:17:51.380
where there isn't a free press

1656
01:17:51.380 --> 01:17:54.390
and where there is a lot of government suppression,

1657
01:17:54.390 --> 01:17:57.070
social media can be incredibly powerful and important.

1658
01:17:57.070 --> 01:17:57.903
Yeah.

1659
01:17:59.820 --> 01:18:03.690
Had you ever met Jane before this project?

1660
01:18:03.690 --> 01:18:04.523
No.

1661
01:18:06.740 --> 01:18:08.363
It turns out, on my flight to Tanzania,

1662
01:18:08.363 --> 01:18:11.425
that Jane was actually like right in front of me,

1663
01:18:11.425 --> 01:18:13.400
in the airline seat right in front of me.

1664
01:18:13.400 --> 01:18:15.170
Oh, no way.
(Doug laughs)

1665
01:18:15.170 --> 01:18:16.003
Wow.

1666
01:18:16.003 --> 01:18:19.430
And I had brought her a bottle of Johnny Walker,

1667
01:18:19.430 --> 01:18:21.143
which I knew was her fave.

1668
01:18:23.340 --> 01:18:26.040
She was sitting there with her Mr. H,

1669
01:18:26.040 --> 01:18:31.040
her little monkey that she was given by a man who is blind

1670
01:18:31.450 --> 01:18:32.810
that she carries around the world,

1671
01:18:32.810 --> 01:18:34.460
and so she was there and I introduced myself

1672
01:18:34.460 --> 01:18:38.300
and I said that was coming and she was very gracious.

1673
01:18:38.300 --> 01:18:40.520
And so she invited me around

1674
01:18:40.520 --> 01:18:44.550
to have dinner with her family the next day

1675
01:18:44.550 --> 01:18:46.900
before we started our interviews.

1676
01:18:46.900 --> 01:18:49.680
And so I gave her the,

1677
01:18:49.680 --> 01:18:51.930
I brought the bottle of Johnny Walker with me

1678
01:18:52.940 --> 01:18:55.513
and she set it on the counter next to this,

1679
01:18:56.410 --> 01:19:00.510
what looked like a two gallon pour-over bottle

1680
01:19:00.510 --> 01:19:01.820
of Famous Grouse whiskey

1681
01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:03.127
that was half gone, and I was like,

1682
01:19:03.127 --> 01:19:05.460
"Oh, damn, this is gonna intense."

1683
01:19:05.460 --> 01:19:07.067
And so I was a bit--

1684
01:19:07.067 --> 01:19:08.180
And she shamed you

1685
01:19:08.180 --> 01:19:09.750
and told you you should've bought the cheaper one, right?

1686
01:19:09.750 --> 01:19:10.969
She did.

1687
01:19:10.969 --> 01:19:12.040
She also told me I shoulda bought the cheaper one

1688
01:19:12.040 --> 01:19:13.923
and donated the money.

1689
01:19:15.706 --> 01:19:18.193
So she is a big fan of whiskey.

1690
01:19:19.195 --> 01:19:21.440
I think it helps her voice

1691
01:19:21.440 --> 01:19:23.420
because she's constantly talking all the time,

1692
01:19:23.420 --> 01:19:25.520
but she also started the tradition

1693
01:19:25.520 --> 01:19:29.057
of what she calls having a wee dram with her.

1694
01:19:29.057 --> 01:19:30.720
Wherever she was in the world,

1695
01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:35.100
she and her mom would kinda toast in the evening together,

1696
01:19:35.100 --> 01:19:37.183
which was a very sweet tradition.

1697
01:19:38.100 --> 01:19:41.480
But obviously her reputation precedes her.

1698
01:19:41.480 --> 01:19:45.190
You go into this with a certain sensibility about who she is

1699
01:19:45.190 --> 01:19:46.410
and what she's gonna be like.

1700
01:19:46.410 --> 01:19:50.000
Like, was there anything about her that surprised you

1701
01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:52.780
or that was different than what you had read

1702
01:19:52.780 --> 01:19:57.250
or kind of consumed in all the media that there is about,

1703
01:19:57.250 --> 01:19:59.140
a footage of her doing her thing?

1704
01:19:59.140 --> 01:20:00.020
Well, there are a couple of things.

1705
01:20:00.020 --> 01:20:02.640
I mean, I think that just seeing,

1706
01:20:02.640 --> 01:20:07.640
that in this 87-year-old woman was like a military general.

1707
01:20:12.527 --> 01:20:17.523
She's very sweet, very kind, but she's tough as nails.

1708
01:20:19.284 --> 01:20:20.380
You don't get to be Jane Goodall,

1709
01:20:20.380 --> 01:20:25.290
you don't go into the forest without some serious will

1710
01:20:25.290 --> 01:20:28.903
and some serious kind of sense of yourself.

1711
01:20:29.934 --> 01:20:33.250
And so that was really powerful and amazing to see,

1712
01:20:33.250 --> 01:20:35.480
her power and her strength,

1713
01:20:35.480 --> 01:20:40.480
as kind of soft, as gently as it is deployed.

1714
01:20:42.750 --> 01:20:44.760
Like, you shake the Dalai Lama's hand

1715
01:20:44.760 --> 01:20:47.348
and you feel like you're about to be thrown across the room

1716
01:20:47.348 --> 01:20:50.750
in a martial arts move,

1717
01:20:50.750 --> 01:20:52.790
like there's power and strength in that.

1718
01:20:52.790 --> 01:20:55.830
And even in Archbishop Tutu's hand,

1719
01:20:55.830 --> 01:20:59.420
which is feeble by polio,

1720
01:20:59.420 --> 01:21:02.070
you feel the strength and the power there

1721
01:21:02.070 --> 01:21:03.750
and I think that's really inspiring,

1722
01:21:03.750 --> 01:21:08.750
is just seeing somebody that isn't fading away.

1723
01:21:08.770 --> 01:21:12.625
If anything, they're like Yoda or something.

1724
01:21:12.625 --> 01:21:15.320
They're becoming more powerful.

1725
01:21:15.320 --> 01:21:16.520
So that was really amazing.

1726
01:21:16.520 --> 01:21:20.330
The other thing that I was really impressed with

1727
01:21:20.330 --> 01:21:25.330
was that she has this kind of seeker's willingness

1728
01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:29.930
to ask the big questions, like, about life and its meaning.

1729
01:21:29.930 --> 01:21:31.850
A lot of scientists, they're kind of focused

1730
01:21:31.850 --> 01:21:35.630
on their little expertise and their little area of study,

1731
01:21:35.630 --> 01:21:38.860
I mean, not that little, but their specific expertise

1732
01:21:38.860 --> 01:21:40.730
and they're not really willing to stray from that.

1733
01:21:40.730 --> 01:21:43.860
She's willing to ask the biggest questions in life

1734
01:21:43.860 --> 01:21:46.170
and she's got this scientist willingness

1735
01:21:46.170 --> 01:21:49.473
to follow the facts wherever they may lead.

1736
01:21:50.510 --> 01:21:54.210
One of the core kind of things about her

1737
01:21:54.210 --> 01:21:59.210
is this idea of empathy in science, right,

1738
01:21:59.770 --> 01:22:02.300
which was really kind of radical when she was starting.

1739
01:22:02.300 --> 01:22:06.180
Like, you have to have empathy to be a good scientist,

1740
01:22:06.180 --> 01:22:08.760
which was at odds with this notion that no,

1741
01:22:08.760 --> 01:22:11.530
empathy is at odds with the scientific method.

1742
01:22:11.530 --> 01:22:12.420
Right, right.

1743
01:22:12.420 --> 01:22:15.210
And having that, like, conviction about that,

1744
01:22:15.210 --> 01:22:17.560
as a young person, is amazing.

1745
01:22:17.560 --> 01:22:21.160
Like, just in, she's naming all the chimps and the apes

1746
01:22:21.160 --> 01:22:23.669
and everyone's telling her, "Don't do that."

1747
01:22:23.669 --> 01:22:24.502
Right.

1748
01:22:24.502 --> 01:22:27.950
And just her kind of seeing things as clearly as she did

1749
01:22:27.950 --> 01:22:30.670
and the conviction that she was on the right path, like,

1750
01:22:30.670 --> 01:22:32.340
is really remarkable.

1751
01:22:32.340 --> 01:22:36.860
And I also, to your point of like having that gravitas,

1752
01:22:36.860 --> 01:22:39.715
like she's always quoting Winston Churchill.

1753
01:22:39.715 --> 01:22:40.724
(all laugh)

1754
01:22:40.724 --> 01:22:42.120
So it's like clearly,

1755
01:22:42.120 --> 01:22:44.050
and being a child of the war and all of that,

1756
01:22:44.050 --> 01:22:45.523
like that looms large.

1757
01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:48.360
What I take from that is,

1758
01:22:48.360 --> 01:22:50.310
like, she knows that she's a leader

1759
01:22:50.310 --> 01:22:53.410
and she's looking to the greatest leaders

1760
01:22:53.410 --> 01:22:56.690
for her own inspiration about how best to lead

1761
01:22:56.690 --> 01:22:58.890
and how to maintain conviction

1762
01:22:58.890 --> 01:23:02.030
in the face of tremendous obstacles.

1763
01:23:02.030 --> 01:23:03.940
Well, this kinda transitions us

1764
01:23:03.940 --> 01:23:05.250
to the fourth reason of hope,

1765
01:23:05.250 --> 01:23:06.780
of the indomitable human spirit,

1766
01:23:06.780 --> 01:23:09.670
which obviously Churchill is one of her examples,

1767
01:23:09.670 --> 01:23:12.760
and I think she is, I mean, that was one of my interests,

1768
01:23:12.760 --> 01:23:14.040
the things I wanted to understand,

1769
01:23:14.040 --> 01:23:16.730
was her own indomitable human spirit,

1770
01:23:16.730 --> 01:23:18.300
and where does that come from,

1771
01:23:18.300 --> 01:23:21.650
and that resilience that you spoke of before

1772
01:23:21.650 --> 01:23:26.650
and that sense of kind of really not being stopped

1773
01:23:27.460 --> 01:23:31.630
by either the seeming facts or the biases

1774
01:23:31.630 --> 01:23:33.480
and the prejudices of the time

1775
01:23:33.480 --> 01:23:36.523
that would say a woman wasn't able to do what she did,

1776
01:23:37.490 --> 01:23:42.470
that ability to really be indomitable.

1777
01:23:42.470 --> 01:23:46.720
I mean, she really, you can see that in her.

1778
01:23:46.720 --> 01:23:49.020
And I think that was really fascinating.

1779
01:23:49.020 --> 01:23:50.440
When we were having that conversation

1780
01:23:50.440 --> 01:23:54.380
about how she really feels like there's something,

1781
01:23:54.380 --> 01:23:57.060
what she calls this indomitable human spirit

1782
01:23:57.060 --> 01:24:02.060
that is both deep in our own individual self

1783
01:24:02.090 --> 01:24:07.090
and our own capacity for endurance and survival

1784
01:24:08.730 --> 01:24:12.710
and meeting the challenges that we face

1785
01:24:12.710 --> 01:24:14.990
and that it's something bigger than us,

1786
01:24:14.990 --> 01:24:18.200
that it's not just like, as she said, it's also,

1787
01:24:18.200 --> 01:24:22.780
if we don't get there, others will pick up the baton

1788
01:24:22.780 --> 01:24:24.750
and finish the race.

1789
01:24:24.750 --> 01:24:27.490
And that's this, so there's this almost

1790
01:24:27.490 --> 01:24:31.000
kinda transpersonal piece of it, which was really inspiring,

1791
01:24:31.000 --> 01:24:34.500
of like it's not ours to finish the work.

1792
01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:35.640
We need to do our part.

1793
01:24:35.640 --> 01:24:38.160
But if we don't get it done fully,

1794
01:24:38.160 --> 01:24:40.440
there are others who will pick up the cause

1795
01:24:40.440 --> 01:24:42.500
and make it happen.

1796
01:24:42.500 --> 01:24:45.080
Yeah, that reminds me of this quote.

1797
01:24:45.080 --> 01:24:46.770
Do you know Scott Harrison is?

1798
01:24:46.770 --> 01:24:50.229
He has this non-profit called Charity Water.

1799
01:24:50.229 --> 01:24:51.062
Oh yes, ah-huh.

1800
01:24:51.062 --> 01:24:51.895
But he always says like,

1801
01:24:51.895 --> 01:24:54.930
"Do not be afraid of work that has no end."

1802
01:24:54.930 --> 01:24:56.600
Like, it's not about the destination;

1803
01:24:56.600 --> 01:24:59.260
it's about devoting yourself to solving a problem,

1804
01:24:59.260 --> 01:25:02.520
even with the understanding that in your lifetime

1805
01:25:02.520 --> 01:25:04.370
it will likely not be solved.

1806
01:25:04.370 --> 01:25:06.106
Yeah, exactly.

1807
01:25:06.106 --> 01:25:10.250
It reminds me of a story that Bryan Stevenson told me

1808
01:25:10.250 --> 01:25:12.310
in one of the projects

1809
01:25:12.310 --> 01:25:14.320
that we worked on his book "Just Mercy"

1810
01:25:14.320 --> 01:25:16.780
and he talked about how,

1811
01:25:16.780 --> 01:25:19.410
when he met Rosa Parks and he was telling her

1812
01:25:19.410 --> 01:25:22.500
that he was gonna be working on mass incarceration reform

1813
01:25:22.500 --> 01:25:25.457
and getting people off of death row, and she's like,

1814
01:25:25.457 --> 01:25:28.767
"Ooh, Bryan, that's gonna make you tired, tired, tired."

1815
01:25:28.767 --> 01:25:30.170
(Doug laughs)

1816
01:25:30.170 --> 01:25:32.640
It's like, yeah, we can't,

1817
01:25:32.640 --> 01:25:36.470
if we think about it as it's just that,

1818
01:25:36.470 --> 01:25:37.640
I think one of the things

1819
01:25:37.640 --> 01:25:41.990
that our whole kinda educational system,

1820
01:25:41.990 --> 01:25:45.870
our whole society makes it all about us

1821
01:25:45.870 --> 01:25:49.870
and our own ego and our own success or failure

1822
01:25:49.870 --> 01:25:53.610
and our own, what Archbishop Tutu calls, self-regard.

1823
01:25:53.610 --> 01:25:57.040
And what I learned from the Dalai Lama and Tutu about joy

1824
01:25:57.040 --> 01:25:59.190
is that it's like, there's so much more joy

1825
01:25:59.190 --> 01:26:01.620
when we go beyond our own self-regard,

1826
01:26:01.620 --> 01:26:03.610
when we go beyond our own sense

1827
01:26:03.610 --> 01:26:05.380
of like fixating on ourselves.

1828
01:26:05.380 --> 01:26:07.130
So like when I was in the room

1829
01:26:07.130 --> 01:26:09.210
before the interviews with them,

1830
01:26:09.210 --> 01:26:12.530
thinking like, "Who the hell am I to do these interviews,"

1831
01:26:12.530 --> 01:26:15.083
that's where misery comes from.

1832
01:26:16.000 --> 01:26:17.490
Hopelessness comes from.

1833
01:26:17.490 --> 01:26:19.910
When I realized like I'm just there

1834
01:26:19.910 --> 01:26:23.310
to do what needs to be done in that room

1835
01:26:23.310 --> 01:26:26.730
so that what's meant to happen can happen,

1836
01:26:26.730 --> 01:26:29.910
then there's something much greater than us

1837
01:26:29.910 --> 01:26:33.500
that settles into us and gives us this incredible strength

1838
01:26:33.500 --> 01:26:36.090
and power that we're talking about with people like Jane

1839
01:26:36.090 --> 01:26:39.700
or Bryan Stevenson or Archbishop Tutu or any of them,

1840
01:26:39.700 --> 01:26:41.120
but even in our own lives

1841
01:26:41.120 --> 01:26:43.520
it'll give us this kind of superpower

1842
01:26:43.520 --> 01:26:47.090
because we realize it's not about us.

1843
01:26:47.090 --> 01:26:49.880
We're here to help something much greater,

1844
01:26:49.880 --> 01:26:52.050
whether that's our kids and our family

1845
01:26:52.050 --> 01:26:54.520
or whether that's getting our society

1846
01:26:54.520 --> 01:26:56.910
to the place that it needs to go

1847
01:26:56.910 --> 01:27:00.210
to be able to survive and thrive on this planet

1848
01:27:00.210 --> 01:27:02.760
or whether it's about developing

1849
01:27:02.760 --> 01:27:05.110
a bipartisan reality in our society

1850
01:27:05.110 --> 01:27:08.160
that allows us to solve the big problems that we have.

1851
01:27:08.160 --> 01:27:11.550
When we are able to go beyond

1852
01:27:11.550 --> 01:27:16.373
just the kind of like fixated on our own either

1853
01:27:18.340 --> 01:27:23.340
our own bank account or our own survival or our own success,

1854
01:27:24.370 --> 01:27:28.260
there's that indomitable human spirit, I think, comes in

1855
01:27:28.260 --> 01:27:31.610
even more powerfully because it becomes bigger.

1856
01:27:31.610 --> 01:27:33.900
We become bigger--
Percolate up, yeah, yeah.

1857
01:27:33.900 --> 01:27:36.840
It's not my natural predisposition.

1858
01:27:36.840 --> 01:27:38.220
Like, it requires work.

1859
01:27:38.220 --> 01:27:40.280
It's like I'm caught up in my own bullshit

1860
01:27:40.280 --> 01:27:42.177
and I'm thinking about like how does this affect me

1861
01:27:42.177 --> 01:27:46.170
and like where am I gonna have dinner or whatever.

1862
01:27:46.170 --> 01:27:49.220
But I think that example that you just shared

1863
01:27:49.220 --> 01:27:50.180
is so powerful.

1864
01:27:50.180 --> 01:27:51.850
Like, when you're in that situation

1865
01:27:51.850 --> 01:27:55.110
and you're just about to go have conversations

1866
01:27:55.110 --> 01:28:00.110
with two of the biggest global icons in modern history,

1867
01:28:00.490 --> 01:28:03.957
it's easy to get caught up and thinking about like,

1868
01:28:03.957 --> 01:28:06.650
"Oh, I'm gonna screw this up," or like, "Why am I here,"

1869
01:28:06.650 --> 01:28:08.100
or like, "This is all wrong.

1870
01:28:08.100 --> 01:28:09.300
They've made a mistake."

1871
01:28:10.300 --> 01:28:12.653
When is Oprah or Anderson Cooper coming?

1872
01:28:12.653 --> 01:28:13.486
I know.

1873
01:28:13.486 --> 01:28:15.467
And it's like, why can't the default just be like,

1874
01:28:15.467 --> 01:28:16.650
"I'm here for a reason.

1875
01:28:16.650 --> 01:28:18.390
Like, I don't know what led me to this point,

1876
01:28:18.390 --> 01:28:21.450
but there are forces, invisible forces, at work

1877
01:28:21.450 --> 01:28:22.790
that have put me in this position,

1878
01:28:22.790 --> 01:28:25.820
so just allow me to be a channel or a vessel

1879
01:28:25.820 --> 01:28:26.840
for the greatest good.

1880
01:28:26.840 --> 01:28:29.240
Like, allow whatever happens now

1881
01:28:29.240 --> 01:28:33.880
to be beyond like my personal self-will

1882
01:28:35.780 --> 01:28:39.640
so that it could be, the greater than the sum of its parts,

1883
01:28:39.640 --> 01:28:41.090
so to speak," right?
Right.

1884
01:28:41.090 --> 01:28:44.940
And that requires kind of like a surrender and a humility

1885
01:28:46.250 --> 01:28:47.830
and a getting out of the way

1886
01:28:47.830 --> 01:28:51.360
and a kind of sensibility of allowing

1887
01:28:51.360 --> 01:28:56.010
that is not, like, natural but can be cultivated.

1888
01:28:56.010 --> 01:28:58.710
And I just know in my own personal experience.

1889
01:28:58.710 --> 01:29:00.940
When I approach situations

1890
01:29:00.940 --> 01:29:04.920
and I'm able to live in that space, that it's always better.

1891
01:29:04.920 --> 01:29:06.450
And it's also more gratifying,

1892
01:29:06.450 --> 01:29:10.390
because your, when you can kind of transcend

1893
01:29:10.390 --> 01:29:13.590
your selfish personal desires

1894
01:29:13.590 --> 01:29:15.970
and be in a space of service and giving,

1895
01:29:15.970 --> 01:29:17.340
the result is always better

1896
01:29:17.340 --> 01:29:19.220
and everything actually ends up

1897
01:29:19.220 --> 01:29:21.751
working out a lot better anyway.

1898
01:29:21.751 --> 01:29:23.104
You know what I meant?

1899
01:29:23.104 --> 01:29:25.780
Yeah, I think that it's, when you say, it's interesting,

1900
01:29:25.780 --> 01:29:30.000
like it's not natural, I would say that it's not encouraged

1901
01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:32.920
in our society to think that way.

1902
01:29:32.920 --> 01:29:36.510
It's all about our grades and our career and our success.

1903
01:29:36.510 --> 01:29:39.190
And look, somehow life evolved

1904
01:29:39.190 --> 01:29:42.980
for this kind of separate locomotion and we,

1905
01:29:42.980 --> 01:29:45.070
are these individual people,

1906
01:29:45.070 --> 01:29:48.950
and we see the world through our own eyes and we,

1907
01:29:48.950 --> 01:29:53.650
are the director and hero of our own movie,

1908
01:29:53.650 --> 01:29:56.980
24/7, that's how our consciousness works

1909
01:29:56.980 --> 01:29:59.020
and we're not getting away from that either.

1910
01:29:59.020 --> 01:30:01.660
But I think that when,

1911
01:30:01.660 --> 01:30:03.860
like there's this interesting thing you were talking about,

1912
01:30:03.860 --> 01:30:06.474
about the difference between hope and faith

1913
01:30:06.474 --> 01:30:09.210
of like, well, a belief structure

1914
01:30:09.210 --> 01:30:11.530
that says there's something greater going on here,

1915
01:30:11.530 --> 01:30:14.910
that there is a greater purpose to life

1916
01:30:15.862 --> 01:30:18.373
I think can be hugely sustaining.

1917
01:30:20.027 --> 01:30:22.620
Or a belief in God, for many people,

1918
01:30:22.620 --> 01:30:24.820
can be really, really, really powerful.

1919
01:30:24.820 --> 01:30:26.660
And hope is a little different than that.

1920
01:30:26.660 --> 01:30:31.227
Like faith says, "Okay, I know these things to be true."

1921
01:30:32.390 --> 01:30:37.390
Hope says like, "I hope these things are true."

1922
01:30:40.280 --> 01:30:41.657
It's self-defining.

1923
01:30:41.657 --> 01:30:43.760
It's humbler in some way.

1924
01:30:43.760 --> 01:30:45.263
It's not so confident.

1925
01:30:47.132 --> 01:30:50.540
But I do think that, to come back to the point about

1926
01:30:50.540 --> 01:30:55.160
whether it's the challenge of getting out of our own

1927
01:30:55.160 --> 01:30:57.570
kind of self-absorption and our,

1928
01:30:57.570 --> 01:31:02.290
like I am as self-absorbed as anyone,

1929
01:31:02.290 --> 01:31:05.920
I mean, I've got the same, like the same rumination,

1930
01:31:05.920 --> 01:31:09.760
the same kind of self-doubts as everyone that has

1931
01:31:09.760 --> 01:31:12.770
and we're not getting, again, getting rid of those things.

1932
01:31:12.770 --> 01:31:16.980
I think it's like can we step into a state, right,

1933
01:31:16.980 --> 01:31:20.893
not necessarily, it's not necessarily,

1934
01:31:21.880 --> 01:31:25.190
I think some of these people who do devote their lives

1935
01:31:25.190 --> 01:31:29.150
to something greater than themselves develop it as a trait,

1936
01:31:29.150 --> 01:31:31.120
but can you step into this state

1937
01:31:31.120 --> 01:31:32.980
where you're able to set aside

1938
01:31:33.973 --> 01:31:37.330
this sense of isolation, fragmentation,

1939
01:31:37.330 --> 01:31:40.690
alienation, separateness and step in,

1940
01:31:40.690 --> 01:31:44.610
back into that tapestry of life that Jane describes

1941
01:31:44.610 --> 01:31:47.840
and feel a part of something greater

1942
01:31:47.840 --> 01:31:50.470
and recognize that we have that capacity

1943
01:31:50.470 --> 01:31:51.530
to be something greater?

1944
01:31:51.530 --> 01:31:52.500
That is, I think,

1945
01:31:52.500 --> 01:31:55.230
when we tap into that indomitable human spirit.

1946
01:31:55.230 --> 01:31:57.960
I think the indomitable human spirit can happen

1947
01:31:57.960 --> 01:31:59.183
in our own survival.

1948
01:32:01.460 --> 01:32:04.790
Every survival movie is an example

1949
01:32:04.790 --> 01:32:07.260
of the indomitable human spirit and our will to survive,

1950
01:32:07.260 --> 01:32:08.170
but it's a little different.

1951
01:32:08.170 --> 01:32:09.750
I asked her, "It's a little different

1952
01:32:09.750 --> 01:32:10.950
than the will to survive.

1953
01:32:10.950 --> 01:32:15.370
It is something greater than just our own individual will

1954
01:32:15.370 --> 01:32:17.720
to keep living."

1955
01:32:17.720 --> 01:32:19.810
Yeah, because the will to survive

1956
01:32:19.810 --> 01:32:23.310
might compel you to opt out of whatever that solution is

1957
01:32:23.310 --> 01:32:24.610
out of self-preservation.

1958
01:32:24.610 --> 01:32:27.410
So there's a selfless aspect to it,

1959
01:32:27.410 --> 01:32:29.870
like the human, the indomitable human spirit,

1960
01:32:29.870 --> 01:32:32.400
like being called upon to perhaps

1961
01:32:32.400 --> 01:32:34.770
even put yourself in danger for the greater good.

1962
01:32:34.770 --> 01:32:36.800
Exactly, which people have been doing

1963
01:32:36.800 --> 01:32:38.900
throughout all human history

1964
01:32:38.900 --> 01:32:41.670
for something greater than themselves.

1965
01:32:41.670 --> 01:32:42.543
It's interesting.

1966
01:32:44.610 --> 01:32:47.350
When we're talking about the indomitable human spirit

1967
01:32:47.350 --> 01:32:50.550
and kind of our own life and the end of our life,

1968
01:32:50.550 --> 01:32:52.560
obviously Jane is 87,

1969
01:32:52.560 --> 01:32:56.893
and we had this fascinating conservation about death.

1970
01:32:58.062 --> 01:33:00.477
And I don't know if you, if we should talk about that.

1971
01:33:00.477 --> 01:33:02.060
No, let's do it.

1972
01:33:02.060 --> 01:33:04.410
But I think that that was even

1973
01:33:04.410 --> 01:33:06.570
really interesting and hopeful.

1974
01:33:06.570 --> 01:33:11.100
I asked her what her next great adventure was going to be,

1975
01:33:11.100 --> 01:33:12.600
and she said death.

1976
01:33:12.600 --> 01:33:16.140
And I had this image of Jane Goodall

1977
01:33:16.140 --> 01:33:18.860
with the binoculars and the notepad

1978
01:33:18.860 --> 01:33:21.327
heading out into the afterlife like,

1979
01:33:21.327 --> 01:33:23.337
"Okay, what are we gonna find here?"

1980
01:33:24.820 --> 01:33:28.040
But it was fascinating to hear her views on the afterlife.

1981
01:33:28.040 --> 01:33:30.360
Yeah, so expound upon that a little bit,

1982
01:33:30.360 --> 01:33:31.760
because it is interesting.

1983
01:33:31.760 --> 01:33:34.780
I mean, she's obviously contemplating this deeply,

1984
01:33:34.780 --> 01:33:39.780
as she inches closer and closer to this inevitable end.

1985
01:33:39.910 --> 01:33:42.380
But I think she has such a healthy

1986
01:33:44.330 --> 01:33:47.223
and thoughtful approach to it.

1987
01:33:48.380 --> 01:33:49.780
Not just similar from your father.

1988
01:33:49.780 --> 01:33:54.080
Yeah, I mean, it really is this kind of view that,

1989
01:33:54.080 --> 01:33:55.840
when my dad was dying, he said,

1990
01:33:55.840 --> 01:33:58.383
I asked him how long he was gonna stick around,

1991
01:34:00.930 --> 01:34:02.350
and he said, "I'm just waiting here

1992
01:34:02.350 --> 01:34:04.570
for landing instructions."

1993
01:34:04.570 --> 01:34:07.485
It's unbelievable, yeah

1994
01:34:07.485 --> 01:34:09.240
He had this view that there was

1995
01:34:09.240 --> 01:34:11.563
some kind of consciousness after death.

1996
01:34:12.470 --> 01:34:14.380
I was really skeptical about that again.

1997
01:34:14.380 --> 01:34:16.160
I mean, maybe it's just because we rebel

1998
01:34:16.160 --> 01:34:18.490
against what our parents' kinda view.

1999
01:34:18.490 --> 01:34:20.720
And I think he actually came to that in his life.

2000
01:34:20.720 --> 01:34:22.620
I don't know if that was always there.

2001
01:34:23.550 --> 01:34:28.183
But Jane had this similar view, and here we're going to,

2002
01:34:29.670 --> 01:34:32.550
we're going from hope to faith, right,

2003
01:34:32.550 --> 01:34:34.100
we're going to beliefs,

2004
01:34:34.100 --> 01:34:39.100
but this view that we go on into another realm

2005
01:34:39.520 --> 01:34:42.633
and that our consciousness goes on beyond the grave.

2006
01:34:45.216 --> 01:34:46.630
She shares, in the book,

2007
01:34:46.630 --> 01:34:48.890
we share some of her incredible stories

2008
01:34:48.890 --> 01:34:50.980
of when her husband died

2009
01:34:50.980 --> 01:34:54.130
or other stories that she's experienced

2010
01:34:54.130 --> 01:34:56.370
or knows of that speak to that.

2011
01:34:56.370 --> 01:34:58.590
And as my dad was dying,

2012
01:34:58.590 --> 01:35:01.120
we were also working with a guy named Bruce Greyson,

2013
01:35:01.120 --> 01:35:04.480
who wrote a book called "After," which is about,

2014
01:35:04.480 --> 01:35:08.550
he's a guy who, kind of the world's leader and expert

2015
01:35:08.550 --> 01:35:10.280
on near-death experience,

2016
01:35:10.280 --> 01:35:13.140
people who've kinda come close to death

2017
01:35:13.140 --> 01:35:15.880
and kinda peeked over but then come back.

2018
01:35:15.880 --> 01:35:20.100
And even Alexander, who wrote "Proof of Heaven,"

2019
01:35:20.100 --> 01:35:22.170
went to, try to get some understanding

2020
01:35:22.170 --> 01:35:24.400
of what the hell had happened to him.

2021
01:35:24.400 --> 01:35:25.520
He went to Bruce Greyson

2022
01:35:25.520 --> 01:35:28.700
and Bruce Greyson looked at his medical charts and--

2023
01:35:28.700 --> 01:35:31.391
Was he the guy who was like a surgeon?

2024
01:35:31.391 --> 01:35:34.140
So Eben Alexander was the guy who was a surgeon, yes.

2025
01:35:34.140 --> 01:35:34.973
Yeah, yeah, I know that story.

2026
01:35:34.973 --> 01:35:37.940
Right, and so Eben Alexander went to Bruce Greyson

2027
01:35:37.940 --> 01:35:41.060
to kind of figure out what had happened to him

2028
01:35:41.060 --> 01:35:43.320
when he had this near-death experience.

2029
01:35:43.320 --> 01:35:46.760
And so Bruce Greyson spent the last 40 years

2030
01:35:46.760 --> 01:35:48.740
developing the field of near-death studies

2031
01:35:48.740 --> 01:35:52.890
and looking at thousands of these cases of,

2032
01:35:52.890 --> 01:35:56.400
and one of the things that was fascinating to have him say

2033
01:35:56.400 --> 01:35:59.210
was that in all, like all of those,

2034
01:35:59.210 --> 01:36:01.510
from looking at all those cases,

2035
01:36:01.510 --> 01:36:04.150
there's an enormous amount of commonality.

2036
01:36:04.150 --> 01:36:08.350
And they're fundamentally based on that kind of,

2037
01:36:08.350 --> 01:36:10.370
so it's not an experimental science,

2038
01:36:10.370 --> 01:36:11.370
they don't kill some people

2039
01:36:11.370 --> 01:36:14.395
or put them in near-death experience--

2040
01:36:14.395 --> 01:36:15.228
Yeah, inherently anecdotal.

2041
01:36:15.228 --> 01:36:18.606
It's anecdotal, it's observational science like astronomy.

2042
01:36:18.606 --> 01:36:20.530
It's observational science.

2043
01:36:20.530 --> 01:36:23.830
But from observing all those people's experience,

2044
01:36:23.830 --> 01:36:25.450
what he's come to the conclusion

2045
01:36:25.450 --> 01:36:28.000
is that there is consciousness after death,

2046
01:36:28.000 --> 01:36:30.323
that our consciousness goes on in some way.

2047
01:36:31.330 --> 01:36:33.253
And Jane clearly felt that way too.

2048
01:36:37.350 --> 01:36:39.973
Watching my dad die and kinda being there,

2049
01:36:41.060 --> 01:36:44.980
fortunate enough, to hold his hand as he was transitioning,

2050
01:36:44.980 --> 01:36:48.890
which many of us have not had a chance to do during COVID,

2051
01:36:48.890 --> 01:36:52.080
it was, you start wondering.

2052
01:36:52.080 --> 01:36:54.980
It's like being pushed up to the front of the row

2053
01:36:56.130 --> 01:36:58.500
and you just start thinking about death

2054
01:36:58.500 --> 01:37:01.250
and the meaning of life in a whole different way.

2055
01:37:01.250 --> 01:37:05.838
So has that shifted your cynical New York mind?

2056
01:37:05.838 --> 01:37:06.877
Yeah (laughs).

2057
01:37:08.480 --> 01:37:11.130
The New York in me says, "Hey, it could be possible."

2058
01:37:12.740 --> 01:37:13.980
There's a chance.

2059
01:37:13.980 --> 01:37:15.258
All you can hope.

2060
01:37:15.258 --> 01:37:16.361
You can hope, right, exactly.

2061
01:37:16.361 --> 01:37:17.740
There's a possibility.

2062
01:37:17.740 --> 01:37:22.660
And like none of us will know until we get there.

2063
01:37:22.660 --> 01:37:26.123
And Jane's gonna probably get there before many of us.

2064
01:37:27.435 --> 01:37:31.383
And maybe this'll send some notes back from the field--

2065
01:37:33.400 --> 01:37:34.480
Right.

2066
01:37:34.480 --> 01:37:36.690
But what I would say

2067
01:37:36.690 --> 01:37:40.810
is I think that I've come to feel like,

2068
01:37:40.810 --> 01:37:43.370
whether it's our own curriculum

2069
01:37:43.370 --> 01:37:46.100
that we're creating in our own life

2070
01:37:46.100 --> 01:37:49.610
or it is some greater sense of purpose

2071
01:37:49.610 --> 01:37:53.480
or indomitable human spirit that goes beyond our own life

2072
01:37:53.480 --> 01:37:57.130
and binds us together from generation to generation

2073
01:37:57.130 --> 01:38:02.130
and across generation that there is this human project

2074
01:38:02.700 --> 01:38:05.760
that we are up to, that we are trying to figure out

2075
01:38:05.760 --> 01:38:08.870
how to be a welcome species on this planet,

2076
01:38:08.870 --> 01:38:11.680
how to survive and thrive in a way

2077
01:38:11.680 --> 01:38:16.680
that has become more urgent now than ever before.

2078
01:38:19.610 --> 01:38:24.600
And it's an incredibly exciting time to be alive

2079
01:38:24.600 --> 01:38:26.577
and it's not unlike World War II

2080
01:38:26.577 --> 01:38:31.010
and that we have got to become the greatest generations

2081
01:38:31.010 --> 01:38:33.640
that have ever lived if we're gonna face these challenges.

2082
01:38:33.640 --> 01:38:34.550
Sure.

2083
01:38:34.550 --> 01:38:37.630
And on the subject of consciousness and faith,

2084
01:38:37.630 --> 01:38:39.080
I mean, the way that I interpreted

2085
01:38:39.080 --> 01:38:42.500
where Jane was coming from is that it's a choice,

2086
01:38:42.500 --> 01:38:44.210
like you can choose.

2087
01:38:44.210 --> 01:38:46.050
She's like, "Look, either nothing happens

2088
01:38:46.050 --> 01:38:47.060
or something happens.

2089
01:38:47.060 --> 01:38:49.240
I'm gonna choose to believe that something happens,"

2090
01:38:49.240 --> 01:38:51.780
and then she tells these amazing stories

2091
01:38:51.780 --> 01:38:54.700
of coincidences that have occurred throughout her life,

2092
01:38:54.700 --> 01:38:57.150
like this amazing story of coming up from the beach

2093
01:38:57.150 --> 01:38:58.597
when the bombing raids were going on

2094
01:38:58.597 --> 01:39:01.740
and her mom takes unlikely route

2095
01:39:01.740 --> 01:39:03.240
that she's never taken before

2096
01:39:03.240 --> 01:39:05.900
and where they would typically walk like a bomb explodes,

2097
01:39:05.900 --> 01:39:08.630
like her life is peppered with a lot of these stories.

2098
01:39:08.630 --> 01:39:11.010
And I think if we're all honest with ourselves,

2099
01:39:11.010 --> 01:39:14.180
we can all find weird synchronous things

2100
01:39:14.180 --> 01:39:16.300
that have happened in our lives and coincidences.

2101
01:39:16.300 --> 01:39:18.640
And we have a choice like, "Are those meaningful?

2102
01:39:18.640 --> 01:39:22.440
Can we look at those and extract some wisdom

2103
01:39:22.440 --> 01:39:25.630
or develop some level of awe and wander

2104
01:39:25.630 --> 01:39:27.220
about the mysteries of life,"

2105
01:39:27.220 --> 01:39:31.190
or are we just gonna say, "Well, that's just coincidence,"

2106
01:39:31.190 --> 01:39:33.240
and kind of move on from there.

2107
01:39:33.240 --> 01:39:36.830
And I think what's beautiful about Jane's example

2108
01:39:36.830 --> 01:39:40.960
and her testimony and her lived experience

2109
01:39:40.960 --> 01:39:45.960
is that because she is so immersed in the interconnectedness

2110
01:39:46.040 --> 01:39:51.020
and the tapestry of life, like so wedded to it,

2111
01:39:51.020 --> 01:39:56.020
she's able to appreciate the mysteries that that provokes.

2112
01:39:57.380 --> 01:40:01.140
Like, the humility that's required to understand,

2113
01:40:01.140 --> 01:40:02.570
like we don't have all the answers

2114
01:40:02.570 --> 01:40:04.490
and there's so much more going on and we've only,

2115
01:40:04.490 --> 01:40:07.720
she says like, "We've only begun to even understand

2116
01:40:07.720 --> 01:40:10.760
the first aspects of what is actually happening."

2117
01:40:10.760 --> 01:40:15.480
And with that humility, I think there's so much opportunity

2118
01:40:15.480 --> 01:40:18.040
and space to have faith.

2119
01:40:18.040 --> 01:40:21.810
Like the more you know, the more crazy and mysterious it is.

2120
01:40:21.810 --> 01:40:23.253
Right, exactly.

2121
01:40:23.253 --> 01:40:27.080
And I think that lots of the most visionary scientists,

2122
01:40:27.080 --> 01:40:28.690
we got to work with Stephen Hawking,

2123
01:40:28.690 --> 01:40:30.290
who, at the end of his life,

2124
01:40:30.290 --> 01:40:35.290
and they're, I think they're able to kinda peer there,

2125
01:40:35.430 --> 01:40:38.150
they recognize that humility of what we don't know

2126
01:40:38.150 --> 01:40:39.110
and the mystery of it.

2127
01:40:39.110 --> 01:40:43.230
And obviously Einstein was very famously--

2128
01:40:43.230 --> 01:40:44.780
She talks about him, yeah.
Talks about that.

2129
01:40:44.780 --> 01:40:49.440
And I think that one of the interesting distinctions

2130
01:40:49.440 --> 01:40:52.800
is between hope and optimism.

2131
01:40:52.800 --> 01:40:57.800
And so optimism is more often a kind of disposition

2132
01:40:57.960 --> 01:40:58.890
or a philosophy.

2133
01:40:58.890 --> 01:41:00.310
Whereas hope, I think,

2134
01:41:00.310 --> 01:41:03.130
is this thing that you actually have to engage in.

2135
01:41:03.130 --> 01:41:04.810
You have to cultivate.

2136
01:41:04.810 --> 01:41:06.130
You have to nurture it.

2137
01:41:06.130 --> 01:41:07.640
You have to pursue it.

2138
01:41:07.640 --> 01:41:11.250
You have to take action to encourage it, to have more of it.

2139
01:41:11.250 --> 01:41:14.500
And Christiana Figueres, who we were talking about

2140
01:41:14.500 --> 01:41:16.340
who did the Paris climate treaty,

2141
01:41:16.340 --> 01:41:18.440
has her version of kind of hope

2142
01:41:18.440 --> 01:41:20.443
or what she calls stubborn optimism.

2143
01:41:21.389 --> 01:41:23.550
And I think what she explained

2144
01:41:23.550 --> 01:41:26.363
was getting the Paris climate treaty done,

2145
01:41:27.549 --> 01:41:31.040
you had to put optimism and hope into the process.

2146
01:41:31.040 --> 01:41:35.530
You had to create the imaginative possibility for people

2147
01:41:35.530 --> 01:41:37.290
that this could be done, right?

2148
01:41:37.290 --> 01:41:39.943
So we're often sitting around there, saying,

2149
01:41:41.157 --> 01:41:42.360
"Am I hopeful today?

2150
01:41:42.360 --> 01:41:43.870
What do the facts look like?

2151
01:41:43.870 --> 01:41:46.230
Am I feeling optimistic?"

2152
01:41:46.230 --> 01:41:48.330
Like, "No, it's gonna rain.

2153
01:41:48.330 --> 01:41:49.397
I'm not hopeful."

2154
01:41:50.560 --> 01:41:54.320
What I think she's saying to us, which is so powerful,

2155
01:41:54.320 --> 01:41:56.500
and I think Jane is saying the same thing,

2156
01:41:56.500 --> 01:41:58.380
which is actually, "No,"

2157
01:41:58.380 --> 01:42:03.140
it's, "You actually don't wait for the facts to be hopeful;

2158
01:42:03.140 --> 01:42:07.670
you actually create the facts that then generate the hope."

2159
01:42:07.670 --> 01:42:10.020
Yeah, that's the opposite of what you would think.

2160
01:42:10.020 --> 01:42:12.340
Yeah.
That's so interesting.

2161
01:42:12.340 --> 01:42:17.340
Can you be a hopeful pessimist?

2162
01:42:17.428 --> 01:42:19.761
(all laugh)

2163
01:42:21.890 --> 01:42:24.170
I think when we were researching optimism

2164
01:42:24.170 --> 01:42:26.293
and pessimism and hope like,

2165
01:42:27.281 --> 01:42:29.270
it seems like there's just a certain,

2166
01:42:29.270 --> 01:42:31.650
temperamentally, some people are more optimistic

2167
01:42:31.650 --> 01:42:32.690
or more pessimistic.

2168
01:42:32.690 --> 01:42:34.510
It's not clear how much of that is nature

2169
01:42:34.510 --> 01:42:36.930
and how much of that is nurture, right?

2170
01:42:36.930 --> 01:42:38.610
Some of that is trauma actually,

2171
01:42:38.610 --> 01:42:41.620
these people who've kind of had their hopes dashed

2172
01:42:41.620 --> 01:42:46.010
and are kind of in defensive postures as a result of that.

2173
01:42:46.010 --> 01:42:50.100
Some people, it's probably because that, just their wiring,

2174
01:42:50.100 --> 01:42:51.990
that they're more biased towards that,

2175
01:42:51.990 --> 01:42:56.040
that negative bias and they're kind of more oriented

2176
01:42:56.040 --> 01:42:58.240
towards being vigilant,

2177
01:42:58.240 --> 01:43:00.900
and that's probably necessary for our species too.

2178
01:43:00.900 --> 01:43:02.423
I mean, that's the other thing when you look at it,

2179
01:43:02.423 --> 01:43:04.810
when you see the diversity of human minds.

2180
01:43:04.810 --> 01:43:06.980
And speaking as a dyslexic here,

2181
01:43:06.980 --> 01:43:10.930
we need all of the minds, we need all of their diversity.

2182
01:43:10.930 --> 01:43:14.720
And so some people probably have that

2183
01:43:14.720 --> 01:43:17.600
more pessimistic disposition.

2184
01:43:17.600 --> 01:43:20.240
But I think that's why this is not dispositional.

2185
01:43:20.240 --> 01:43:23.070
This is about what Jane calls hope,

2186
01:43:23.070 --> 01:43:27.840
a fundamental human survival trait without which we perish.

2187
01:43:27.840 --> 01:43:29.730
It's this capacity, like language.

2188
01:43:29.730 --> 01:43:32.440
You don't have to develop that language capacity.

2189
01:43:32.440 --> 01:43:34.550
We have the ability for language.

2190
01:43:34.550 --> 01:43:35.920
But if you don't cultivate it,

2191
01:43:35.920 --> 01:43:38.382
then you're never gonna learn language.

2192
01:43:38.382 --> 01:43:41.600
And that hope is this thing that we have

2193
01:43:41.600 --> 01:43:44.970
that now we have some way of understanding

2194
01:43:44.970 --> 01:43:46.410
how we can cultivate it,

2195
01:43:46.410 --> 01:43:48.480
how we can nurture it in one another,

2196
01:43:48.480 --> 01:43:50.510
how we can sustain and support it in one another.

2197
01:43:50.510 --> 01:43:54.700
Yeah, how does that translate into parenting?

2198
01:43:54.700 --> 01:43:57.000
Like, we're now in a situation

2199
01:43:57.000 --> 01:43:59.810
where we have this new generation of young people.

2200
01:43:59.810 --> 01:44:01.390
We're both parents.

2201
01:44:01.390 --> 01:44:06.390
And this generation is inheriting this climate crisis.

2202
01:44:06.920 --> 01:44:10.640
And with that, there's a distrust of the Gen Xers

2203
01:44:10.640 --> 01:44:12.750
and the Boomers and all of that.

2204
01:44:12.750 --> 01:44:14.793
There's perhaps a little bit of anger,

2205
01:44:15.640 --> 01:44:20.160
a little bit of despair, a little bit of self-righteousness,

2206
01:44:20.160 --> 01:44:22.870
and also kind of a mental health epidemic--

2207
01:44:22.870 --> 01:44:23.810
Totally.
That's precipitated

2208
01:44:23.810 --> 01:44:25.621
by the pandemic and social media

2209
01:44:25.621 --> 01:44:27.250
and everything else that's going on.

2210
01:44:27.250 --> 01:44:32.250
So how do you instill hope in the next generation

2211
01:44:33.230 --> 01:44:38.230
as a parent trying to help guide young people to maturity?

2212
01:44:38.240 --> 01:44:39.543
Such a great question.

2213
01:44:40.480 --> 01:44:42.960
I think there is a mental health pandemic

2214
01:44:42.960 --> 01:44:45.890
that's going on now and I think young people

2215
01:44:45.890 --> 01:44:48.673
are kinda disproportionately affected by it.

2216
01:44:49.820 --> 01:44:54.820
I think that the rampant kind of cases of depression,

2217
01:44:57.080 --> 01:45:00.440
anxiety, both clinically and subclinically

2218
01:45:00.440 --> 01:45:03.230
is a huge crisis in our world.

2219
01:45:03.230 --> 01:45:06.060
I think one of the things to say is that

2220
01:45:06.060 --> 01:45:11.060
I think this is the way that the crises in our world

2221
01:45:12.780 --> 01:45:15.700
are expressing themselves through people

2222
01:45:15.700 --> 01:45:18.140
and through our children, right?

2223
01:45:18.140 --> 01:45:21.380
It's like, again, we like to see this as individualized

2224
01:45:21.380 --> 01:45:26.190
and itemized and somehow like my child's mental health

2225
01:45:26.190 --> 01:45:30.080
is somehow separate from the state of the planet

2226
01:45:30.080 --> 01:45:31.560
and the health of the planet

2227
01:45:31.560 --> 01:45:35.260
or that because it feels, "Okay, I can give my child Prozac

2228
01:45:35.260 --> 01:45:36.663
or deal with this."

2229
01:45:37.560 --> 01:45:40.130
And yes, obviously we need to deal

2230
01:45:40.130 --> 01:45:42.203
with our kids' challenges.

2231
01:45:43.160 --> 01:45:44.140
My kids have talked to me

2232
01:45:44.140 --> 01:45:46.570
about whether they wanna have kids or not

2233
01:45:46.570 --> 01:45:50.370
because there's so much despair out there about the world

2234
01:45:50.370 --> 01:45:52.360
that our children are inheriting.

2235
01:45:52.360 --> 01:45:55.053
And I think that we really have,

2236
01:45:56.270 --> 01:45:59.430
we have not done what we needed to do

2237
01:45:59.430 --> 01:46:03.970
to be good ancestors for our kids and grandkids

2238
01:46:03.970 --> 01:46:05.473
and great grandchildren.

2239
01:46:06.660 --> 01:46:09.753
I think that, that said, I think,

2240
01:46:11.010 --> 01:46:13.840
coming back to this idea of hope as a social gift,

2241
01:46:13.840 --> 01:46:18.840
is like we can model hope in our own lives.

2242
01:46:18.940 --> 01:46:23.550
Obviously as a parent, what we do is way more important

2243
01:46:23.550 --> 01:46:24.443
than what we say.

2244
01:46:25.530 --> 01:46:28.190
So our own relationship to life

2245
01:46:28.190 --> 01:46:30.660
and our own relationship to the future

2246
01:46:30.660 --> 01:46:35.180
and how hopeful we are, I think, is really significant.

2247
01:46:35.180 --> 01:46:38.047
I think that supporting them

2248
01:46:38.047 --> 01:46:42.230
and also that social support that helps them

2249
01:46:42.230 --> 01:46:45.270
in their times of crisis is huge and important

2250
01:46:45.270 --> 01:46:49.813
and like the most important thing.

2251
01:46:50.890 --> 01:46:53.550
And at the same time one of the things that,

2252
01:46:53.550 --> 01:46:56.300
the hardest lesson I have had as a parent

2253
01:46:56.300 --> 01:46:59.130
is realize that I cannot save my children

2254
01:46:59.130 --> 01:47:03.520
from their suffering and that it's actually not my job

2255
01:47:03.520 --> 01:47:05.960
to take away their suffering.

2256
01:47:05.960 --> 01:47:09.050
Their suffering is actually how they grow

2257
01:47:09.050 --> 01:47:11.660
and develop what sculpts their soul.

2258
01:47:11.660 --> 01:47:15.920
And what I can do is be in the suffering with them

2259
01:47:15.920 --> 01:47:20.570
when requested, but try to take it away from them.

2260
01:47:20.570 --> 01:47:23.700
And I think that that's the other piece of like resilience

2261
01:47:23.700 --> 01:47:27.510
and of active hope, is like it's not about

2262
01:47:27.510 --> 01:47:29.940
being a helicopter parent or a bulldozer parent

2263
01:47:29.940 --> 01:47:34.880
or trying to solve all the problems or make the path easy.

2264
01:47:34.880 --> 01:47:37.210
Coming back to that fundamental recognition

2265
01:47:37.210 --> 01:47:40.270
that human life is filled with challenges.

2266
01:47:40.270 --> 01:47:44.830
You look at people like Tutu and the Dalai Lama and Jane,

2267
01:47:44.830 --> 01:47:47.190
and you're like, "Wow, these lives are amazing

2268
01:47:47.190 --> 01:47:50.050
because they're filled with adversity."

2269
01:47:50.050 --> 01:47:53.220
This wasn't like some unending yoga class that they were in

2270
01:47:53.220 --> 01:47:58.150
that it's like blissful from start to finish, right?

2271
01:47:58.150 --> 01:48:02.060
And actually when you, coming to that phrase from my dad,

2272
01:48:02.060 --> 01:48:05.170
if we see this as a part of our curriculum,

2273
01:48:05.170 --> 01:48:09.800
then even the anxiety and the despair and the depression

2274
01:48:09.800 --> 01:48:12.770
that's part of the curriculum too, that is part of the work.

2275
01:48:12.770 --> 01:48:16.430
And I mean, I think having grown up with a mom

2276
01:48:16.430 --> 01:48:18.790
who suffered from depression, I think,

2277
01:48:18.790 --> 01:48:20.790
I thought for a long time that my job

2278
01:48:20.790 --> 01:48:25.790
was to kinda run away from sadness and ran after joy.

2279
01:48:25.900 --> 01:48:27.450
And working on "The Book of Joy"

2280
01:48:27.450 --> 01:48:28.840
with Tutu and the Dalai Lama,

2281
01:48:28.840 --> 01:48:32.410
I realized there ain't no joy without sadness

2282
01:48:32.410 --> 01:48:36.310
and there's no joy without sorrow

2283
01:48:36.310 --> 01:48:40.480
and that those two things are intricately connected.

2284
01:48:40.480 --> 01:48:45.170
And so helping our kids to experience the pain,

2285
01:48:45.170 --> 01:48:48.730
I guess this is the other thing I would say, is grief.

2286
01:48:48.730 --> 01:48:53.200
Like recognizing, you were so griephobic in this society.

2287
01:48:53.200 --> 01:48:58.200
But we're experiencing enormous grief as a society

2288
01:48:58.350 --> 01:49:01.980
and we grief for all we've lost in the pandemic,

2289
01:49:01.980 --> 01:49:04.630
grief for what's happening in our world.

2290
01:49:04.630 --> 01:49:06.840
I mean, there's this amazing story that we tell

2291
01:49:06.840 --> 01:49:10.710
in "The Book of Hope" about this woman and her,

2292
01:49:10.710 --> 01:49:12.400
who's working with the Inuit people

2293
01:49:12.400 --> 01:49:15.840
and her relationship to grief, which we can talk about,

2294
01:49:15.840 --> 01:49:19.030
but I think that recognizing

2295
01:49:19.030 --> 01:49:20.820
that we have to grieve our losses

2296
01:49:20.820 --> 01:49:25.820
and that there's healing in that if we grief that

2297
01:49:25.880 --> 01:49:28.020
and are able to be there in that suffering

2298
01:49:28.020 --> 01:49:29.073
and don't deny it.

2299
01:49:30.860 --> 01:49:33.000
Yeah, our culture isn't exactly

2300
01:49:33.000 --> 01:49:34.770
permissive to that process,

2301
01:49:34.770 --> 01:49:38.040
and yet that's the only path towards wholeness.

2302
01:49:38.040 --> 01:49:39.810
Like, you have to feel those feelings.

2303
01:49:39.810 --> 01:49:41.300
You have to go through it.

2304
01:49:41.300 --> 01:49:42.610
You can't short circuit it.

2305
01:49:42.610 --> 01:49:45.120
You can't repress it.

2306
01:49:45.120 --> 01:49:46.446
So hard, man.

2307
01:49:46.446 --> 01:49:47.279
(Doug laughs)

2308
01:49:47.279 --> 01:49:49.830
That impulse of like wanting to make it okay for your kids,

2309
01:49:49.830 --> 01:49:50.820
like I just wanna--
Yeah, oh my God.

2310
01:49:50.820 --> 01:49:52.440
I wanna alleviate the pain.

2311
01:49:52.440 --> 01:49:55.330
I wanna swoop in and make it okay.

2312
01:49:55.330 --> 01:49:57.710
And the sense that, like,

2313
01:49:57.710 --> 01:49:59.790
this world that they're inheriting,

2314
01:49:59.790 --> 01:50:04.640
like this, it's a real heavy kinda vibe

2315
01:50:04.640 --> 01:50:07.700
that they shoulder for themselves.

2316
01:50:07.700 --> 01:50:12.380
And you feel like the culprit in that on some level.

2317
01:50:12.380 --> 01:50:16.372
Well, I was just down with Archbishop Tutu in South Africa

2318
01:50:16.372 --> 01:50:19.660
and we were talking about when our kids suffer

2319
01:50:19.660 --> 01:50:22.610
and he said it's like a double blow,

2320
01:50:22.610 --> 01:50:26.530
because you suffer because they're suffering

2321
01:50:26.530 --> 01:50:29.453
and you feel responsible in some way you could've,

2322
01:50:30.570 --> 01:50:32.400
you're responsible in some way for that suffering.

2323
01:50:32.400 --> 01:50:36.020
Yeah, to the extent that you helped create it

2324
